Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · adjacency matrices · access · L2
Concept: Out-degree is the row sum and in-degree is the column sum
Misconception: The out-degree of u is the sum of column u.
Explanation: Row u lists the targets of edges leaving u, so the out-degree is the sum of cells (u, 0..n-1). Column v lists the sources of edges entering v, which gives the in-degree. In an undirected graph the matrix is symmetric and both sums are equal. Each degree costs O(V).
Incorrect:
size_t amOutDegreeBad(const unsigned char *a, size_t n, size_t u) {
  size_t d = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) d += a[i * n + u];
  return d;
}
Correct:
size_t amOutDegree(const AdjMatrix *g, size_t u) {
  size_t d = 0;
  if (u >= g->n) return 0;
