Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · adjacency matrices · iteration · L3
Concept: Listing the neighbors of a vertex scans a whole row, O(V), even for a sparse graph
Misconception: Visiting the neighbors of u takes time proportional to its degree.
Explanation: The matrix has no list of neighbors, so the only way to find them is to scan row u and test each cell. A traversal over the whole graph therefore costs O(V^2) in a matrix, against O(V + E) in an adjacency list. That is why matrices suit dense graphs or graphs with small, fixed V.
Incorrect:
size_t amNeighborsBad(const unsigned char *a, size_t n, size_t u, size_t *out) {
  size_t k = 0;
  for (size_t v = 0; v <= n; v++)
    if (a[u * n + v]) out[k++] = v;
  return k;
}
Correct:
size_t amNeighbors(const AdjMatrix *g, size_t u, size_t *out) {
  size_t k = 0;
  if (u >= g->n) return 0;
  for (size_t v = 0; v < g->n; v++)
    if (g->a[u * g->n + v]) out[k++] = v;
  return k;
}
Complexity: O(V)

