C · array · access · L1
Concept: Valid indexes are 0 to n-1; a loop that uses <= n writes one element past the array
Misconception: for (i = 0; i <= n; i++) visits all n elements.
Explanation: An array of n elements has indexes 0 to n-1. Index n is one past the end: the compiler accepts it and the program corrupts neighbouring memory, which is undefined behaviour. AddressSanitizer reports it as a stack-buffer-overflow. Use i < n and pass the length along with the pointer.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow):
void fillBad(int *a, int n) {
  for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) a[i] = i;
}
Correct:
void fillIota(int *a, int n) {
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) a[i] = i;
}
Complexity: O(n)

