Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · array · access · L2
Concept: Two-dimensional arrays are stored row by row, so the flat index is row * cols + col
Misconception: The flat position of m[i][j] is i + j * cols.
Explanation: C stores int m[rows][cols] as rows blocks of cols integers, one after another (row-major order). Element m[i][j] sits at flat index i * cols + j. Using j * cols + i reads a different element, and for non-square arrays it can run past the end.
Incorrect:
int flatBad(int i, int j, int cols) {
  return i + j * cols;
}
Correct:
int flatIndex(int i, int j, int cols) {
  return i * cols + j;
}
Complexity: O(1)

