C · binary trees · transformation · L2
Concept: Mirror a tree by swapping the two children; assigning one child from the result of the other aliases a subtree
Misconception: left = invert(right); right = invert(left) mirrors the node.
Explanation: The second assignment uses the left pointer that the first assignment already replaced, so both children end up pointing at the same subtree and the other one is lost. Swap through a temporary after recursing into both children, or recurse first and then swap.
Incorrect:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
TNode *invertBad(TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return NULL;
  t->left = invertBad(t->right);
  t->right = invertBad(t->left);
  return t;
}
Correct:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
TNode *invertTree(TNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return NULL;
  TNode *l = invertTree(t->left);
  TNode *r = invertTree(t->right);
  t->left = r;
  t->right = l;
  return t;
}
Complexity: O(n)

