Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · binary trees · iteration · L1
Concept: Preorder visits the node first, inorder between the children, postorder last
Misconception: Preorder, inorder and postorder differ only in naming.
Explanation: The three depth-first orders differ in when the node is visited relative to its two recursive calls: before them (preorder), between them (inorder) and after them (postorder). Moving the visit changes the output. Preorder copies a tree, postorder deletes one and evaluates expression trees, and inorder gives sorted output for a search tree.
Incorrect:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
void preorderBad(const TNode *t, int *out, size_t *n) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  preorderBad(t->left, out, n);
  out[(*n)++] = t->val;
  preorderBad(t->right, out, n);
}
Correct:
typedef struct TNode { int val; struct TNode *left, *right; } TNode;
void preorder(const TNode *t, int *out, size_t *n) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  out[(*n)++] = t->val;
  preorder(t->left, out, n);
  preorder(t->right, out, n);
}
void inorder(const TNode *t, int *out, size_t *n) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  inorder(t->left, out, n);
  out[(*n)++] = t->val;
  inorder(t->right, out, n);
}
void postorder(const TNode *t, int *out, size_t *n) {
  if (t == NULL) return;
  postorder(t->left, out, n);
  postorder(t->right, out, n);
  out[(*n)++] = t->val;
}
Complexity: O(n)

