Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · Bloom filters · insertion-and-deletion · L4
Concept: A Bloom filter cannot remove a key; clearing its bits creates false negatives for others, so rebuild instead
Misconception: Removing a key means clearing its k bits.
Explanation: Bits are shared between keys. Clearing the bits of one key can clear a bit another key relies on, and that key then reports not present even though it was added, a false negative, which a Bloom filter must never produce. Use a counting filter, or rebuild: clear all bits and re-add the keys that remain, O(n k).
Incorrect:
void bloomRemoveBad(uint32_t *w, const size_t *idx, unsigned k) {
  for (unsigned i = 0; i < k; i++) w[idx[i] / 32] &= ~(1u << (idx[i] % 32));
}
Correct:
int bloomRebuild(Bloom *b, const char *const *keys, size_t n) {
  if (b->w == NULL) return 0;
  memset(b->w, 0, (b->m + 31) / 32 * sizeof *b->w);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) bloomAdd(b, keys[i]);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n k)

