Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · Bloom filters · complexity · L3
Concept: For n keys and false-positive rate p use m = -n ln p / (ln 2)^2 bits and k = (m / n) ln 2 probes; log10 gives the wrong size
Misconception: The bit count can be computed with log10 instead of the natural logarithm.
Explanation: The optimal size is m = -n ln(p) / (ln 2)^2 and the optimal probe count is k = (m / n) ln 2, both with natural logarithms. Using log10 shrinks m by a factor of about 2.3, so the real false-positive rate is much higher than p. For n = 1000 and p = 0.01 the filter needs 9586 bits and 7 probes, about 1.2 KB, independent of the key length.
Incorrect:
#define BLOOM_LN2 0.69314718055994530942
size_t bloomBitsBad(size_t n, double p) {
  return (size_t)ceil(-(double)n * log10(p) / (BLOOM_LN2 * BLOOM_LN2));
}
Correct:
#define BLOOM_LN2 0.69314718055994530942
size_t bloomBits(size_t n, double p) {
  return (size_t)ceil(-(double)n * log(p) / (BLOOM_LN2 * BLOOM_LN2));
}
unsigned bloomHashes(size_t m, size_t n) {
  double k = (double)m / (double)n * BLOOM_LN2;
  return k < 1.0 ? 1u : (unsigned)(k + 0.5);
}
Complexity: O(1)

