C · binary search trees · testing · L3
Concept: Validate a search tree with ranges; comparing each node only with its children misses violations deeper down
Misconception: A tree is a search tree if every node is greater than its left child and smaller than its right child.
Explanation: The invariant is about whole subtrees: every key in the right subtree must exceed the node, not only the right child. Pass the allowed open interval down the recursion, narrowing it at each step. An inorder walk that checks strictly increasing keys is an equivalent test.
Incorrect:
typedef struct BNode { int key; struct BNode *left, *right; } BNode;
int isBstBad(const BNode *t) {
  if (t == NULL) return 1;
  if (t->left != NULL && t->left->key >= t->key) return 0;
  if (t->right != NULL && t->right->key <= t->key) return 0;
  return isBstBad(t->left) && isBstBad(t->right);
}
Correct:
typedef struct BNode { int key; struct BNode *left, *right; } BNode;
int isBstRange(const BNode *t, long lo, long hi) {
  if (t == NULL) return 1;
  if (t->key <= lo || t->key >= hi) return 0;
  return isBstRange(t->left, lo, t->key) && isBstRange(t->right, t->key, hi);
}
int isBst(const BNode *t) { return isBstRange(t, LONG_MIN, LONG_MAX); }
Complexity: O(n)

