C · B-trees and B+ trees · iteration · L2
Concept: An inorder walk visits child i, then key i, for every i, and finally the last child
Misconception: Print the keys of a node, then recurse into its children.
Explanation: Sorted order interleaves children and keys: everything in child 0, key 0, everything in child 1, key 1, and so on, ending with the last child. Printing a node's keys first and then its children gives a preorder listing, which is not sorted.
Incorrect:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
void btPreorderBad(const BT *x, int *out, size_t *m) {
  if (x == NULL) return;
  for (int i = 0; i < x->n; i++) out[(*m)++] = x->keys[i];
  if (!x->leaf)
    for (int i = 0; i <= x->n; i++) btPreorderBad(x->c[i], out, m);
}
Correct:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
void btInorder(const BT *x, int *out, size_t *m) {
  if (x == NULL) return;
  for (int i = 0; i < x->n; i++) {
    if (!x->leaf) btInorder(x->c[i], out, m);
    out[(*m)++] = x->keys[i];
  }
  if (!x->leaf) btInorder(x->c[x->n], out, m);
}
Complexity: O(n)

