C · B-trees and B+ trees · access · L2
Concept: Search a node by finding the first key >= target, then either match it or descend into the child at that index
Misconception: Scanning the keys of one node is enough to decide membership.
Explanation: A B-tree node holds up to three sorted keys and, if internal, one more child than keys. The child at index i covers the keys between keys[i - 1] and keys[i]. Find i, the first key not smaller than the target; return success on equality, otherwise descend into child i, or stop at a leaf. Reading only the root node misses every key stored lower.
Incorrect:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
int btContainsBad(const BT *x, int k) {
  int i = 0;
  while (i < x->n && k > x->keys[i]) i++;
  return i < x->n && k == x->keys[i];
}
Correct:
typedef struct BT { int n, leaf; int keys[3]; struct BT *c[4]; } BT;
int btContains(const BT *x, int k) {
  while (x != NULL) {
    int i = 0;
    while (i < x->n && k > x->keys[i]) i++;
    if (i < x->n && k == x->keys[i]) return 1;
    if (x->leaf) return 0;
    x = x->c[i];
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(log n)

