Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · double-ended queues · mutation · L3
Concept: Growing a wrapped ring buffer needs a copy in logical order; realloc alone leaves the wrapped part in the wrong place
Misconception: realloc is enough to enlarge a ring buffer.
Explanation: realloc keeps the old slots at the same indexes. If the contents wrapped around the old end, the tail part now precedes the head and the new space sits in the middle of the sequence, so the logical order breaks. Allocate a new block, copy the elements in logical order starting from head, set head to 0 and update the capacity.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t head, len, cap; } Deque;
int dequeGrowBad(Deque *d, size_t ncap) {
  int *p = realloc(d->data, ncap * sizeof *p);
  if (p == NULL) return 0;
  d->data = p;
  d->cap = ncap;
  return 1;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t head, len, cap; } Deque;
int dequeGrow(Deque *d, size_t ncap) {
  int *p = malloc(ncap * sizeof *p);
  if (p == NULL) return 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < d->len; i++) p[i] = d->data[(d->head + i) % d->cap];
  free(d->data);
  d->data = p;
  d->head = 0;
  d->cap = ncap;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

