C · double-ended queues · patterns · L3
Concept: Sliding window maximum: keep a deque of indexes whose values decrease, and evict expired indexes from the front
Misconception: The maximum of every window needs a rescan, O(n*k).
Explanation: Store indexes in a deque with decreasing values. For each new element pop smaller values from the back, push the new index, drop the front index if it left the window, and read the maximum at the front. Every index enters and leaves once, so the total is O(n). Without evicting the front, an old maximum stays after it expired.
Incorrect:
size_t slidingMaxBad(const int *a, size_t n, size_t k, int *out) {
  if (k == 0 || k > n) return 0;
  size_t *dq = malloc(n * sizeof *dq);
  size_t head = 0, tail = 0, m = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    while (head < tail && a[dq[tail - 1]] <= a[i]) tail--;
    dq[tail++] = i;
    if (i + 1 >= k) out[m++] = a[dq[head]];
  }
  free(dq);
  return m;
}
Correct:
size_t slidingMax(const int *a, size_t n, size_t k, int *out) {
  if (k == 0 || k > n) return 0;
  size_t *dq = malloc(n * sizeof *dq);
  if (dq == NULL) return 0;
  size_t head = 0, tail = 0, m = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (head < tail && dq[head] + k <= i) head++;
    while (head < tail && a[dq[tail - 1]] <= a[i]) tail--;
    dq[tail++] = i;
    if (i + 1 >= k) out[m++] = a[dq[head]];
  }
  free(dq);
  return m;
}
Complexity: O(n)

