C · disjoint sets (union-find) · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Check an element against n before find; index n is one past the parent array
Misconception: find(x) can be called with any x because the loop stops at a root.
Explanation: The loop reads parent[x] before it can stop, so x must be a valid index. For x equal to n the first read is already one past the end of the array, an out-of-bounds access that AddressSanitizer reports. The checked dsFind returns DS_NONE for invalid elements, and dsUnion and dsConnected refuse them.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
size_t findBad(const size_t *p, size_t n, size_t x) {
  (void)n;
  while (p[x] != x) x = p[x];
  return x;
}
Correct:
int dsValid(const DSU *d, size_t x) { return x < d->n; }
Complexity: O(1)

