Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · disjoint sets (union-find) · patterns · L4
Concept: An undirected edge closes a cycle when its endpoints already have the same root
Misconception: An undirected graph has a cycle only if some edge has equal endpoints.
Explanation: Process the edges one at a time. If both endpoints already share a root, the edge connects two vertices that are already connected, which closes a cycle. Otherwise union them. A check of u == v finds only self-loops and misses cycles such as 0-1, 1-2, 2-0. The cost is O(m alpha(n)); Kruskal uses the same test while scanning edges by weight.
Incorrect:
int hasCycleBad(const int (*e)[2], size_t m) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < m; i++)
    if (e[i][0] == e[i][1]) return 1;
  return 0;
}
Correct:
int hasCycle(size_t n, const int (*e)[2], size_t m) {
  DSU d;
  int cyc = 0;
  if (!dsInit(&d, n)) return -1;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < m && !cyc; i++) {
    if (e[i][0] < 0 || e[i][1] < 0) continue;
    if (dsConnected(&d, (size_t)e[i][0], (size_t)e[i][1])) cyc = 1;
    else dsUnion(&d, (size_t)e[i][0], (size_t)e[i][1]);
  }
  dsFree(&d);
  return cyc;
}
Complexity: O(m alpha(n))

