Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · disjoint sets (union-find) · creation · L1
Concept: Every element starts as its own root: parent[i] = i, not 0
Misconception: A disjoint set can be initialised by zeroing the parent array.
Explanation: The parent array encodes the forest: an element whose parent is itself is a root and names its set. Initialising every parent to 0 makes all elements children of element 0, so everything is in one set. Use parent[i] = i, rank 0 and a component count of n. Initialisation is O(n).
Incorrect:
void dsInitBad(size_t *p, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i] = 0;
}
Correct:
#define DS_NONE SIZE_MAX
typedef struct { size_t *p; unsigned char *rank; size_t n, count; } DSU;
int dsInit(DSU *d, size_t n) {
  d->p = calloc(n + 1, sizeof *d->p);
  d->rank = calloc(n + 1, 1);
  d->n = n;
  d->count = n;
  if (d->p == NULL || d->rank == NULL) {
    free(d->p);
    free(d->rank);
