Concept: Removing a node you hold a pointer to is O(1), but head and tail must be updated at the ends
Misconception: Unlinking the neighbors is all that removal needs.
Explanation: With prev and next the node can be unlinked without searching: join prev and next to each other. When the node is the head or the tail the list struct must change too; forgetting the tail leaves a dangling pointer that the next append writes through.
