Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · doubly linked lists · transformation · L3
Concept: Reverse a doubly linked list by swapping prev and next in every node, then swap head and tail
Misconception: Redirecting only the next pointers reverses the list.
Explanation: Both pointers of every node must be exchanged, and the saved next must be read before the swap. Afterwards head and tail trade places. Changing only next leaves prev pointing the old way and head and tail unchanged, so the list is corrupt.
Incorrect:
typedef struct DNode { int val; struct DNode *prev, *next; } DNode;
typedef struct { DNode *head, *tail; size_t len; } DList;
void dlReverseBad(DList *l) {
  DNode *p = l->head;
  while (p != NULL) {
    DNode *next = p->next;
    p->next = p->prev;
    p = next;
  }
}
Correct:
typedef struct DNode { int val; struct DNode *prev, *next; } DNode;
typedef struct { DNode *head, *tail; size_t len; } DList;
void dlReverse(DList *l) {
  DNode *p = l->head;
  while (p != NULL) {
    DNode *next = p->next;
    p->next = p->prev;
    p->prev = next;
    p = next;
  }
  DNode *t = l->head;
  l->head = l->tail;
  l->tail = t;
}
Complexity: O(n)

