C · dynamic arrays · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Erase shifts len - i - 1 elements; counting one too many reads past the end
Misconception: Erasing element i moves len - i elements left.
Explanation: Only the elements after i move, which is len - i - 1 of them. Passing len - i makes memmove read one element past the last one. When order does not matter, swap-remove copies the last element into the gap and shrinks the length, which is O(1).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Vec;
void vecEraseBad(Vec *v, size_t i) {
  memmove(&v->data[i], &v->data[i + 1], (v->len - i) * sizeof *v->data);
  v->len--;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Vec;
int vecErase(Vec *v, size_t i) {
  if (i >= v->len) return 0;
  memmove(&v->data[i], &v->data[i + 1], (v->len - i - 1) * sizeof *v->data);
  v->len--;
  return 1;
}
int vecSwapRemove(Vec *v, size_t i) {
  if (i >= v->len) return 0;
  v->data[i] = v->data[v->len - 1];
  v->len--;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

