C · dynamic arrays · access · L3
Concept: Pointers into a dynamic array become invalid when it grows; keep an index and re-read the element
Misconception: A pointer to an element stays valid while the array grows.
Explanation: Growth may move the whole block. A pointer taken before a push then refers to freed memory. Store the index and read v->data[i] after the push, or reserve the needed capacity first so that no reallocation happens.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Vec;
int firstAfterPushBad(Vec *v) {
  int *p = &v->data[0];
  for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) vecPush(v, i);
  return *p;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int *data; size_t len, cap; } Vec;
int firstAfterPush(Vec *v) {
  size_t first = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) vecPush(v, i);
  return v->data[first];
}
Complexity: O(n)

