C · dynamic memory buffers · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Free once: set the pointer to NULL so a second free is harmless
Misconception: Calling free twice on the same pointer is safe.
Explanation: Freeing the same block twice corrupts the allocator and is undefined; sanitizers report double-free. free(NULL) does nothing, so resetting the pointer to NULL right after freeing makes a repeated release a no-op.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: double-free):
void freeTwiceBad(void) {
  int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  free(p);
  free(p);
}
Correct:
void disposeBuffer(int **pp) {
  free(*pp);
  *pp = NULL;
}
int freeTwiceGood(void) {
  int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  disposeBuffer(&p);
  disposeBuffer(&p);
  return p == NULL;
}
Complexity: O(1)

