Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · edge lists · patterns · L4
Concept: Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge n - 1 times and must skip edges whose source is still infinite
Misconception: dist[u] + w can be computed even when dist[u] is INT_MAX.
Explanation: Bellman-Ford handles negative weights by relaxing all edges up to n - 1 times; one extra pass that still improves a distance proves a negative cycle. For an unreachable vertex dist is INT_MAX, and adding a positive weight overflows int, which UBSan reports. Skip any edge whose source distance is infinite. The cost is O(n m) and an edge list is the natural input.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: signed integer overflow):
void bellmanBad(const Edge *e, size_t m, size_t n, int *dist) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) dist[i] = INT_MAX;
  dist[0] = 0;
  for (size_t pass = 0; pass + 1 < n; pass++)
    for (size_t i = 0; i < m; i++)
      if (dist[e[i].u] + e[i].w < dist[e[i].v]) dist[e[i].v] = dist[e[i].u] + e[i].w;
}
Correct:
#define EL_INF INT_MAX
int elBellman(const EdgeList *l, size_t n, size_t src, int *dist) {
  if (src >= n) return 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) dist[i] = EL_INF;
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (size_t pass = 0; pass + 1 < n; pass++) {
    int changed = 0;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < l->m; i++) {
      const Edge *x = &l->e[i];
      if (dist[x->u] == EL_INF) continue;
      if (dist[x->u] + x->w < dist[x->v]) { dist[x->v] = dist[x->u] + x->w; changed = 1; }
    }
    if (!changed) break;
  }
  for (size_t i = 0; i < l->m; i++) {
    const Edge *x = &l->e[i];
    if (dist[x->u] != EL_INF && dist[x->u] + x->w < dist[x->v]) return 0;
  }
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n m)

