C · edge lists · access · L2
Concept: Count degrees by indexing deg[u] and deg[v], after checking both endpoints are below n
Misconception: The endpoints of an edge are always valid indexes into the degree array.
Explanation: An edge list does not know how many vertices there are. Using u or v as an index without checking writes outside a degree array of n entries as soon as one endpoint is n or more, or negative. Validate first and report failure. A self-loop adds 2 to its vertex. Counting is O(m).
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
void degreesBad(const Edge *e, size_t m, size_t *deg) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < m; i++) { deg[e[i].u]++; deg[e[i].v]++; }
}
Correct:
int elDegrees(const EdgeList *l, size_t n, size_t *deg) {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) deg[i] = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < l->m; i++) {
    if (l->e[i].u < 0 || (size_t)l->e[i].u >= n) return 0;
    if (l->e[i].v < 0 || (size_t)l->e[i].v >= n) return 0;
    deg[l->e[i].u]++;
    deg[l->e[i].v]++;
  }
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(m)

