Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · edge lists · patterns · L4
Concept: Kruskal sorts the edges and takes an edge only if its endpoints are in different components
Misconception: The minimum spanning tree is the first n - 1 edges of the sorted list.
Explanation: Taking the n - 1 lightest edges can include a cycle, which wastes an edge and leaves part of the graph disconnected. Kruskal scans the sorted edges and keeps an edge only when a union-find structure says its endpoints are in different components, stopping after n - 1 edges. If fewer than n - 1 edges are kept the graph is disconnected. The cost is O(m log m).
Incorrect:
long long kruskalBad(const Edge *sorted, size_t m, size_t n) {
  long long total = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n - 1 && i < m; i++) total += sorted[i].w;
  return total;
}
Correct:
static int root(int *p, int x) {
  while (p[x] != x) { p[x] = p[p[x]]; x = p[x]; }
  return x;
}
long long elKruskal(EdgeList *l, size_t n) {
  if (n == 0) return 0;
  int *p = malloc(n * sizeof *p);
  if (p == NULL) return -1;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) p[i] = (int)i;
  elSort(l);
  long long total = 0;
  size_t used = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < l->m && used + 1 < n; i++) {
    int a = root(p, l->e[i].u), b = root(p, l->e[i].v);
    if (a == b) continue;
    p[a] = b;
    total += l->e[i].w;
    used++;
  }
  free(p);
  return used + 1 == n ? total : -1;
}
Complexity: O(m log m)

