C · enum · access · L2
Concept: End an enum with a COUNT enumerator to size tables and validate indexes
Misconception: A name table indexed by an enum value cannot go out of range.
Explanation: An extra last enumerator (DAY_COUNT) equals the number of real values, so it sizes the lookup array and bounds the loop automatically when values are added. Check the index against it before using it: the enum type does not stop callers from passing any int.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: out of bounds):
enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, DAY_COUNT };
static const char *const names[DAY_COUNT] = { "Mon", "Tue", "Wed" };
const char *dayNameBad(int d) { return names[d]; }
Correct:
enum Day { MON, TUE, WED, DAY_COUNT };
static const char *const names[DAY_COUNT] = { "Mon", "Tue", "Wed" };
const char *dayName(int d) { return (d >= 0 && d < DAY_COUNT) ? names[d] : "?"; }
Complexity: O(1)

