C · Fenwick trees (binary indexed trees) · access · L2
Concept: A prefix sum walks down with i -= lowbit(i); a range sum is prefix(r) - prefix(l - 1)
Misconception: The sum of positions l..r is prefix(r) - prefix(l).
Explanation: The prefix sum of positions 1..i adds t[i], then t[i - lowbit(i)], and so on until the index is 0. A range l..r with 1-based indexes is prefix(r) - prefix(l - 1). Subtracting prefix(l) leaves out position l. Both queries are O(log n).
Incorrect:
long long fenRangeBad(const long long *t, size_t l, size_t r) {
  long long hi = 0, lo = 0;
  for (size_t i = r; i > 0; i -= (i & (~i + 1))) hi += t[i];
  for (size_t i = l; i > 0; i -= (i & (~i + 1))) lo += t[i];
  return hi - lo;
}
Correct:
long long fenPrefix(const Fenwick *f, size_t i) {
  long long s = 0;
  if (i > f->n) i = f->n;
  for (; i > 0; i -= lowbit(i)) s += f->t[i];
  return s;
}
long long fenRange(const Fenwick *f, size_t l, size_t r) {
  if (l == 0 || l > r) return 0;
  return fenPrefix(f, r) - fenPrefix(f, l - 1);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

