C · function pointer · transformation · L3
Concept: Call a function only through its real type; write an adapter instead of casting
Misconception: Casting a function pointer to another signature lets you call it that way.
Explanation: Calling a function through a pointer of an incompatible type is undefined behavior, even if it appears to work on one platform. gcc reports the cast with -Wcast-function-type. When signatures differ, write a small adapter function with the expected signature that calls the real function.
Incorrect (compiles with a warning: cast between incompatible function types):
typedef int (*TwoArg)(int, int);
int twice(int x) { return 2 * x; }
int callBad(void) {
  TwoArg g = (TwoArg)twice;
  return g(4, 0);
}
Correct:
int twice(int x) { return 2 * x; }
int twiceAdapter(int a, int b) {
  (void)b;
  return twice(a);
}
Complexity: O(1)

