C · function pointer · access · L2
Concept: A table of function pointers replaces a switch; check the index before calling
Misconception: ops[i](a, b) is safe for any integer i.
Explanation: An array of function pointers maps an index or opcode to behavior in O(1). The index must be validated first: reading past the end of the table reads garbage and calling it jumps to an arbitrary address. Return a status code and write the result through an out-parameter.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: out of bounds):
typedef int (*BinOp)(int, int);
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }
static const BinOp ops[3] = { add, sub, mul };
int callBad(size_t i, int a, int b) { return ops[i](a, b); }
Correct:
typedef int (*BinOp)(int, int);
int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
int sub(int a, int b) { return a - b; }
int mul(int a, int b) { return a * b; }
static const BinOp ops[3] = { add, sub, mul };
int dispatch(size_t i, int a, int b, int *out) {
  if (i >= 3) return 0;
  *out = ops[i](a, b);
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

