C · hash tables · mental-model · L1
Concept: A hash function must mix every character and its position; summing the characters collides for all anagrams
Misconception: Adding the character codes is a good hash for a string.
Explanation: A hash maps a key to a bucket index through hash % buckets. Summing the characters gives "ab" and "ba" the same value, and many short words land in a few buckets. DJB2 starts at 5381 and computes h = h * 33 + c for each byte, so position matters. Use uint32_t so the overflow wraps by definition.
Incorrect:
uint32_t hashStrBad(const char *s) {
  uint32_t h = 0;
  for (; *s != '\0'; s++) h += (unsigned char)*s;
  return h;
}
Correct:
uint32_t hashStr(const char *s) {
  uint32_t h = 5381;
  for (; *s != '\0'; s++) h = h * 33 + (unsigned char)*s;
  return h;
}
Complexity: O(len)

