Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · binary heaps · ordering · L3
Concept: Heapsort uses a max-heap to sort ascending in place; a min-heap sorts descending
Misconception: Heapsort with a min-heap sorts the array in ascending order.
Explanation: Heapsort builds a max-heap, then repeatedly swaps the maximum into the last free position and shrinks the heap by one. The largest ends up at the end, so the result is ascending. With a min-heap the smallest goes to the end and the array comes out descending. It runs in O(n log n) time and O(1) extra space but is not stable.
Incorrect:
void heapSortBad(int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t start = n / 2; start-- > 0;) {
    size_t i = start;
    for (;;) {
      size_t l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;
      if (l < n && a[l] < a[m]) m = l;
      if (r < n && a[r] < a[m]) m = r;
      if (m == i) break;
      int t = a[m]; a[m] = a[i]; a[i] = t;
      i = m;
    }
  }
  for (size_t end = n; end-- > 1;) {
    int t = a[0]; a[0] = a[end]; a[end] = t;
    size_t i = 0;
    for (;;) {
      size_t l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;
      if (l < end && a[l] < a[m]) m = l;
      if (r < end && a[r] < a[m]) m = r;
      if (m == i) break;
      int u = a[m]; a[m] = a[i]; a[i] = u;
      i = m;
    }
  }
}
Correct:
void maxDown(int *a, size_t n, size_t i) {
  for (;;) {
    size_t l = 2 * i + 1, r = l + 1, m = i;
    if (l < n && a[l] > a[m]) m = l;
    if (r < n && a[r] > a[m]) m = r;
    if (m == i) break;
    int t = a[m]; a[m] = a[i]; a[i] = t;
    i = m;
  }
}
void heapSort(int *a, size_t n) {
  for (size_t i = n / 2; i-- > 0;) maxDown(a, n, i);
  for (size_t end = n; end-- > 1;) {
    int t = a[0]; a[0] = a[end]; a[end] = t;
    maxDown(a, end, 0);
  }
}
Complexity: O(n log n)

