Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · pointer · iteration · L1
Concept: p + n moves n elements, not n bytes; the compiler scales by the element size
Misconception: Adding 1 to an int pointer moves it one byte.
Explanation: Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the pointed-to type, so p + 1 on an int * advances sizeof(int) bytes. Casting to char * first makes the step a byte, which lands in the middle of an element. Walk a range with a begin pointer and an end pointer one past the last element.
Incorrect:
int *skipBad(int *p, int n) { return (int *)((char *)p + n); }
Correct:
int *skip(int *p, int n) { return p + n; }
int sumRange(const int *b, const int *e) {
  int s = 0;
  for (; b != e; b++) s += *b;
  return s;
}
Complexity: O(1)

