C · pointer · creation · L2
Concept: Never return the address of a local variable; return heap memory or fill a caller buffer
Misconception: Returning &x from a function hands the caller the variable.
Explanation: A local variable dies when the function returns, so a pointer to it dangles and any use is undefined. Allocate with malloc and document that the caller must free, or accept a destination from the caller. gcc warns with -Wreturn-local-addr.
Incorrect (compiles with a warning: address of local variable):
int *badLocal(void) {
  int x = 5;
  return &x;
}
Correct:
int *makeInt(int v) {
  int *p = malloc(sizeof *p);
  if (p != NULL) *p = v;
  return p;
}
Complexity: O(1)

