C · priority queues · ordering · L3
Concept: Break ties with an insertion sequence number so equal priorities leave in FIFO order
Misconception: A binary heap returns equal priorities in the order they were pushed.
Explanation: A heap is not stable. With equal keys the order in which items come out depends on how the sift operations move them: pushing ids 1, 2, 3 with the same priority can pop as 1, 3, 2. Give every item a sequence number when it is pushed and compare (key, seq) so ties resolve first in, first out. The cost is one extra int per item.
Incorrect:
void popOrderBad(int ids[3]) {
  PQ q;
  Item it;
  pqInit(&q, itemLessKeyOnly);
  for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) pqPush(&q, 5, i);
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { pqPop(&q, &it); ids[i] = it.id; }
  pqFree(&q);
}
Correct:
void popOrder(int ids[3]) {
  PQ q;
  Item it;
  pqInit(&q, itemLess);
  for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) pqPush(&q, 5, i);
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { pqPop(&q, &it); ids[i] = it.id; }
  pqFree(&q);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

