C · queues · fit-and-alternatives · L3
Concept: Build a queue from two stacks: refill the output stack only when it is empty
Misconception: Moving the input stack onto the output stack on every dequeue keeps the order.
Explanation: Push onto the input stack and pop from the output stack. When the output stack is empty, pour the whole input stack into it, which reverses it into FIFO order. Pouring while the output stack still has items puts newer items on top of older ones and breaks the order. Each element moves at most twice, so operations are O(1) amortized.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { int in[64], out[64]; size_t nin, nout; } TwoStackQ;
void tsqPush(TwoStackQ *q, int v) { q->in[q->nin++] = v; }
int tsqPopBad(TwoStackQ *q, int *out) {
  while (q->nin > 0) q->out[q->nout++] = q->in[--q->nin];
  if (q->nout == 0) return 0;
  *out = q->out[--q->nout];
  return 1;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { int in[64], out[64]; size_t nin, nout; } TwoStackQ;
void tsqPush(TwoStackQ *q, int v) { q->in[q->nin++] = v; }
int tsqPop(TwoStackQ *q, int *out) {
  if (q->nout == 0)
    while (q->nin > 0) q->out[q->nout++] = q->in[--q->nin];
  if (q->nout == 0) return 0;
  *out = q->out[--q->nout];
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1) amortized

