Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · red-black trees · access · L2
Concept: Floor search: when a node is not greater than the key, remember it and keep looking right
Misconception: The first node not greater than the key is the floor.
Explanation: The floor of a key is the largest stored key that is not greater than it. At a node with key <= target, that node is a candidate but a larger candidate may be in the right subtree; at a larger node, go left. Returning the first candidate gives a smaller key than the real floor. Ordered trees support this in O(log n); hash tables cannot.
Incorrect:
typedef struct RNode { int key, red; struct RNode *l, *r; } RNode;
int isRed(const RNode *n) { return n != NULL && n->red; }
int rbFloorBad(const RNode *t, int key, int *out) {
  while (t != NULL) {
    if (t->key <= key) { *out = t->key; return 1; }
    t = t->l;
  }
  return 0;
}
Correct:
typedef struct RNode { int key, red; struct RNode *l, *r; } RNode;
int isRed(const RNode *n) { return n != NULL && n->red; }
int rbFloor(const RNode *t, int key, int *out) {
  int found = 0;
  while (t != NULL) {
    if (t->key == key) { *out = key; return 1; }
    if (t->key < key) { *out = t->key; found = 1; t = t->r; }
    else t = t->l;
  }
  return found;
}
Complexity: O(log n)

