Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · singly linked list · mutation · L3
Concept: Unlink a node before freeing it; freeing first leaves a dangling next pointer in the list
Misconception: Calling free on the node is enough to delete it.
Explanation: The predecessor still points at a freed node until it is relinked, and traversal then reads freed memory. Use the Node ** walk: when *pp is the node, set *pp to node->next, then free the node. This also handles deleting the head.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int removeFirstBad(Node *head, int v) {
  for (Node *p = head; p != NULL; p = p->next)
    if (p->val == v) { free(p); return 1; }
  return 0;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int removeFirst(Node **head, int v) {
  for (Node **pp = head; *pp != NULL; pp = &(*pp)->next) {
    if ((*pp)->val == v) {
      Node *dead = *pp;
      *pp = dead->next;
      free(dead);
      return 1;
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

