C · singly linked list · creation · L2
Concept: Appending needs the last link: walk a pointer to the next field so the empty list needs no special case
Misconception: Append by walking to the last node and setting its next; that works for every list.
Explanation: For an empty list there is no last node, so code that starts from head->next crashes on NULL. Walk a Node ** that points at each next field until it points at NULL, then store the new node there. The same code handles the empty list because *head is also such a link. Append costs O(n) without a tail pointer.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: null pointer):
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int pushBackBad(Node *head, int v) {
  Node *p = head;
  while (p->next != NULL) p = p->next;
  p->next = malloc(sizeof *p->next);
  if (p->next == NULL) return 0;
  p->next->val = v;
  p->next->next = NULL;
  return 1;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int pushBack(Node **head, int v) {
  Node **pp = head;
  while (*pp != NULL) pp = &(*pp)->next;
  Node *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
  if (n == NULL) return 0;
  n->val = v;
  n->next = NULL;
  *pp = n;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

