C · singly linked list · iteration · L1
Concept: Loop while the current node is not NULL; testing p->next skips the last node and fails on an empty list
Misconception: for (p = head; p->next; p = p->next) visits every node.
Explanation: The loop condition must be p != NULL. Testing p->next stops one node early, so the last value is missed, and it dereferences NULL when the list is empty. Count and sum are O(n) because a singly linked list has no random access.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: null pointer):
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int listSumBad(const Node *h) {
  int s = 0;
  for (const Node *p = h; p->next != NULL; p = p->next) s += p->val;
  return s;
}
Correct:
typedef struct Node { int val; struct Node *next; } Node;
int listSum(const Node *h) {
  int s = 0;
  for (const Node *p = h; p != NULL; p = p->next) s += p->val;
  return s;
}
size_t listLen(const Node *h) {
  size_t n = 0;
  for (const Node *p = h; p != NULL; p = p->next) n++;
  return n;
}
Complexity: O(n)

