Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · string · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Allocate strlen(a) + strlen(b) + 1 bytes to join two strings
Misconception: malloc(strlen(a) + strlen(b)) is enough to join two strings.
Explanation: The terminator needs one more byte than the visible characters. Without it strcat writes the zero one byte past the allocation, a heap buffer overflow. Compute both lengths once, allocate the sum plus one, copy with memcpy, and free the result when done.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
char *joinBad(const char *a, const char *b) {
  char *r = malloc(strlen(a) + strlen(b));
  strcpy(r, a);
  strcat(r, b);
  return r;
}
Correct:
char *joinGood(const char *a, const char *b) {
  size_t na = strlen(a), nb = strlen(b);
  char *r = malloc(na + nb + 1);
  if (r == NULL) return NULL;
  memcpy(r, a, na);
  memcpy(r + na, b, nb + 1);
  return r;
}
Complexity: O(n+m)

