C · struct · creation · L3
Concept: A flexible array member needs extra bytes in the allocation; sizeof the struct leaves no room
Misconception: malloc(sizeof(struct Vec)) is enough for a struct with a flexible array member.
Explanation: A flexible array member (int data[] at the end) has no size and contributes nothing to sizeof. The allocation must add n * sizeof element. Allocating only sizeof(struct Vec) and then writing data[i] overflows the heap block, which AddressSanitizer reports as a heap-buffer-overflow.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-buffer-overflow):
struct Vec { size_t n; int data[]; };
struct Vec *vecBad(size_t n) {
  struct Vec *v = malloc(sizeof *v);
  v->n = n;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) v->data[i] = (int)i;
  return v;
}
Correct:
struct Vec { size_t n; int data[]; };
struct Vec *vecNew(size_t n) {
  struct Vec *v = malloc(sizeof *v + n * sizeof v->data[0]);
  if (v == NULL) return NULL;
  v->n = n;
  for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) v->data[i] = (int)i;
  return v;
}
Complexity: O(n)

