Concept: sizeof a struct is usually larger than the sum of its members because of alignment padding
Misconception: A struct is exactly as large as its members added together.
Explanation: The compiler aligns each member to a multiple of its own size and pads the end to the alignment of the largest member. struct { char a; int b; char c; } is 12 bytes on common 64-bit targets, not 6. Ordering members from largest to smallest removes most padding, so the same members fit in 8 bytes.
