Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c
C · struct · mutation · L3
Concept: Assigning a struct copies pointer members, not what they point to
Misconception: b = a duplicates the buffer that a.p points to.
Explanation: Struct assignment copies each member bit by bit. For a pointer member that is the address, so both structs now share one buffer. Freeing both frees the same block twice, which AddressSanitizer reports as a double-free. Write a clone function that allocates and copies the pointed-to data.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: double-free):
struct Buf { char *p; size_t n; };
void dupBad(void) {
  struct Buf a = { malloc(8), 8 };
  struct Buf b = a;
  free(a.p);
  free(b.p);
}
Correct:
struct Buf { char *p; size_t n; };
struct Buf bufClone(const struct Buf *a) {
  struct Buf b = { malloc(a->n), a->n };
  if (b.p != NULL) memcpy(b.p, a->p, a->n);
  return b;
}
Complexity: O(n)

