C · time and date structures · transformation · L3
Concept: Add days by editing tm_mday and calling mktime; adding 86400 seconds breaks on daylight saving changes
Misconception: Adding 86400 seconds always moves a date forward one day.
Explanation: On the day clocks change a local day is 23 or 25 hours long, so adding 86400 seconds lands an hour off the intended wall-clock time. Convert with localtime_r, add to tm_mday, set tm_isdst to -1 and call mktime, which normalizes out-of-range fields such as 32 January and finds the right offset.
Incorrect:
time_t addDaysBad(time_t t, int d) { return t + (time_t)d * 86400; }
Correct:
time_t addDays(time_t t, int d) {
  struct tm tm;
  localtime_r(&t, &tm);
  tm.tm_mday += d;
  tm.tm_isdst = -1;
  return mktime(&tm);
}
Complexity: O(1)

