C · time and date structures · access · L2
Concept: strftime returns 0 when the buffer is too small; check it before using the text
Misconception: strftime always leaves a usable string in the buffer.
Explanation: strftime returns the number of characters written without the terminator, or 0 if the result did not fit. In the failure case the buffer contents are unspecified. Size the buffer for the format, test the return value and handle failure explicitly.
Incorrect:
int fmtBad(char *buf, size_t cap, const struct tm *t) {
  strftime(buf, cap, "%Y-%m-%d", t);
  return 1;
}
Correct:
int fmtDate(char *buf, size_t cap, const struct tm *t) {
  if (strftime(buf, cap, "%Y-%m-%d", t) == 0) {
    if (cap > 0) buf[0] = '\0';
    return 0;
  }
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(n)

