C · tries (prefix trees) · patterns · L3
Concept: The longest stored word that prefixes a text is the last end flag seen on the path, not the depth reached
Misconception: The deepest node reached while walking the text gives the longest matching word.
Explanation: The path may continue past the last complete word: with "car" and "cargo" stored, the text "carg" walks four levels but the longest word that is a prefix of it is "car". Remember the position of the most recent end flag. Tokenizers and IP routing tables use this longest-match rule.
Incorrect:
typedef struct TrieNode { struct TrieNode *next[26]; int end; } TrieNode;
TrieNode *trieNew(void) { return calloc(1, sizeof(TrieNode)); }
size_t trieLongestBad(const TrieNode *root, const char *text) {
  const TrieNode *t = root;
  size_t depth = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; text[i] != '\0'; i++) {
    if (text[i] < 'a' || text[i] > 'z') break;
    t = t->next[text[i] - 'a'];
    if (t == NULL) break;
    depth = i + 1;
  }
  return depth;
}
Correct:
typedef struct TrieNode { struct TrieNode *next[26]; int end; } TrieNode;
TrieNode *trieNew(void) { return calloc(1, sizeof(TrieNode)); }
size_t trieLongest(const TrieNode *root, const char *text) {
  const TrieNode *t = root;
  size_t best = 0;
  for (size_t i = 0; text[i] != '\0'; i++) {
    if (text[i] < 'a' || text[i] > 'z') break;
    t = t->next[text[i] - 'a'];
    if (t == NULL) break;
    if (t->end) best = i + 1;
  }
  return best;
}
Complexity: O(len)

