C · typedef · patterns · L3
Concept: Declare a typedef for an incomplete struct to hide the representation behind functions
Misconception: Public struct fields are the only way to build a counter.
Explanation: An opaque type is declared in the header as typedef struct Counter Counter; with the fields defined only in the source file. Callers hold a Counter * and use functions, so invariants such as never negative cannot be broken from outside, and the layout can change without recompiling clients.
Incorrect:
struct CounterBad { int n; };
Correct:
typedef struct Counter Counter;
struct Counter { int n; };
Counter *counterNew(void) { return calloc(1, sizeof(Counter)); }
void counterInc(Counter *c) { c->n++; }
int counterGet(const Counter *c) { return c->n; }
void counterFree(Counter *c) { free(c); }
Complexity: O(1)

