C · union · transformation · L3
Concept: Reading float bits needs a union or memcpy; a cast converts the value instead
Misconception: (int)f gives the bit pattern of a float.
Explanation: A cast converts the number: (int)1.0f is 1. To see the IEEE-754 bits, store the float in a union with a uint32_t and read the integer member, which C allows, or memcpy the bytes. A pointer cast such as *(uint32_t *)&f breaks the aliasing rules and is undefined behaviour.
Incorrect:
uint32_t bitsBad(float f) {
  return (uint32_t)f;
}
Correct:
uint32_t floatBits(float f) {
  union { float f; uint32_t u; } x;
  x.f = f;
  return x.u;
}
Complexity: O(1)

