C · void pointer · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Casting a byte pointer to int * can break alignment; use memcpy to read values from buffers
Misconception: Any byte address can be read as an int through a cast.
Explanation: An int must sit at an address that is a multiple of its alignment. Casting buf + 1 to int * and reading is undefined behavior even if the hardware tolerates it, and UBSan reports a misaligned load. memcpy into a local int is well defined for any address and compiles to a single load where possible.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: misaligned):
int loadBad(const unsigned char *b) { return *(const int *)(b + 1); }
Correct:
int loadInt(const unsigned char *b) {
  int v;
