Embedded C · STM32_HAL · adc and dac sampling · fit-and-alternatives · Intermediate
Concept: Measure the real supply voltage with the internal reference instead of assuming 3.3 V
Misconception: VDDA is always 3.3 V, so Vref is a constant.
Explanation: The ADC measures the pin voltage as a fraction of VDDA. If the supply sags (battery, regulator drop), every reading scales with it. The internal reference voltage (about 1.2 V) is fixed, and the factory stores its raw reading at 3.3 V as a calibration value. VDDA in millivolts is then 3300 * VREFINT_CAL / VREFINT_DATA. Use the result as the Vref for the conversion. The calibration address and the value are listed in the datasheet of your part.
Incorrect:
uint32_t vddaBad(uint16_t cal, uint16_t data) {
  (void)cal; (void)data;
  return 3300;
}
Correct:
uint32_t vddaMv(uint16_t vrefintCal, uint16_t vrefintData) {
  return 3300U * vrefintCal / vrefintData;
}
Complexity: O(1)

