Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · avr timer1 and timer0 · mutation · Intermediate
Concept: Guard a multi-byte read with an atomic block that restores the previous interrupt state
Misconception: cli() before and sei() after is a safe critical section.
Explanation: A 32-bit counter takes several instructions to read on an 8-bit AVR, so an interrupt in the middle can give a torn value. Disabling interrupts around the read fixes that, but a plain sei() afterwards turns interrupts on even if they were already off, for example when the function is called from another critical section or from an ISR. ATOMIC_BLOCK(ATOMIC_RESTORESTATE) saves SREG and puts it back.
Incorrect:
volatile uint32_t msBad;
uint32_t readBad(void) {
  uint32_t v;
  cli();
  v = msBad;
  sei();
  return v;
}
Correct:
volatile uint32_t msTicks;
ISR(TIMER1_COMPA_vect) {
  msTicks++;
}
uint32_t millisNow(void) {
  uint32_t v;
  ATOMIC_BLOCK(ATOMIC_RESTORESTATE) {
    v = msTicks;
  }
  return v;
}
Complexity: O(1)

