Concept: Expand a 16-bit UUID into the 128-bit base UUID in little-endian byte order
Misconception: The bytes of a UUID are stored in the order in which it is written.
Explanation: A 16-bit UUID such as 0x180F stands for 0000180F-0000-1000-8000-00805F9B34FB. The Bluetooth stack stores the 128-bit value least significant byte first, so the array begins FB 34 9B 5F and the 16-bit value sits in bytes 12 and 13, low byte first: 0F 18. Writing the value high byte first swaps those bytes and the peer sees UUID 0x0F18.
Incorrect:
void uuidBad(uint16_t u, uint8_t o[16]) {
  static const uint8_t base[12] = {
    0xFB, 0x34, 0x9B, 0x5F, 0x80, 0x00, 0x00, 0x80, 0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x00};
  memcpy(o, base, 12);
  o[12] = (uint8_t)(u >> 8); o[13] = (uint8_t)(u & 0xFFU);
  o[14] = 0; o[15] = 0;
}
Correct:
void uuidExpand(uint16_t u, uint8_t o[16]) {
  static const uint8_t base[12] = {
    0xFB, 0x34, 0x9B, 0x5F, 0x80, 0x00, 0x00, 0x80, 0x00, 0x10, 0x00, 0x00};
  memcpy(o, base, 12);
  o[12] = (uint8_t)(u & 0xFFU); o[13] = (uint8_t)(u >> 8);
  o[14] = 0; o[15] = 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

