Embedded C · STM32_HAL · can bus · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Decide which of two identifiers wins arbitration by comparing bits from the most significant end
Misconception: The frame with the larger identifier has higher priority.
Explanation: CAN is wired so that a dominant bit (0) overrides a recessive bit (1). Nodes send the identifier most significant bit first and a node that sends a 1 but reads a 0 drops out. So the numerically lowest identifier wins. Two nodes must never send the same identifier at the same time. The loop compares bit 10 down to bit 0 and the first difference decides.
Incorrect:
int arbBad(uint16_t a, uint16_t b) {
  return a > b ? 0 : 1;
}
Correct:
int canArb(uint16_t a, uint16_t b) {
  for (int i = 10; i >= 0; i--) {
    int ba = (a >> i) & 1, bb = (b >> i) & 1;
    if (ba != bb) return ba ? 1 : 0;
  }
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

