Embedded C · STM32_HAL · can bus · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Read a 32-bit little-endian value from the data bytes of a frame without signed overflow
Misconception: A byte shifted left by 24 is safe because the result is stored in an unsigned variable.
Explanation: A uint8_t is promoted to int before it is shifted, so d[3] << 24 is a signed shift. For a byte of 0x80 or more the result does not fit in a 32-bit int, which is undefined behaviour even though the value is then stored in an unsigned variable. Cast to uint32_t before shifting. The data bytes of a frame are an array, so the order (little- or big-endian) is a protocol choice and not a property of the CPU.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: left shift):
uint32_t get32Bad(const uint8_t *d) {
  return d[0] | (d[1] << 8) | (d[2] << 16) | (d[3] << 24);
}
Correct:
uint32_t canGet32(const uint8_t *d) {
  return (uint32_t)d[0] | ((uint32_t)d[1] << 8)
       | ((uint32_t)d[2] << 16) | ((uint32_t)d[3] << 24);
}
void canPut32(uint8_t *d, uint32_t v) {
  for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) d[i] = (uint8_t)(v >> (8 * i));
}
Complexity: O(1)

