Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · can bus · transformation · Advanced
Concept: Derive the bit timing register value from the peripheral clock, baud rate and segment lengths
Misconception: The bit timing register stores the segment lengths exactly as you choose them.
Explanation: A CAN bit is split into time quanta: one synchronisation quantum, then segment BS1, then segment BS2. The baud rate is pclk / (prescaler x (1 + BS1 + BS2)). The sample point is at (1 + BS1) / (1 + BS1 + BS2) of the bit, commonly 75 to 87 percent. In the bxCAN BTR register every field is stored minus one (prescaler, BS1, BS2, SJW), so writing the plain values makes every segment one quantum too long and the bus speed wrong. Return zero when the prescaler does not divide evenly.
Incorrect:
uint32_t btrBad(uint32_t pclk, uint32_t baud, unsigned bs1, unsigned bs2, unsigned sjw) {
  uint32_t tq = 1U + bs1 + bs2;
  if (pclk % (baud * tq)) return 0;
  uint32_t pre = pclk / (baud * tq);
  return (sjw << 24) | (bs2 << 20) | (bs1 << 16) | pre;
}
Correct:
uint32_t canBtr(uint32_t pclk, uint32_t baud, unsigned bs1, unsigned bs2, unsigned sjw) {
  uint32_t tq = 1U + bs1 + bs2;
  if (pclk % (baud * tq)) return 0;
  uint32_t pre = pclk / (baud * tq);
  return ((sjw - 1U) << 24) | ((bs2 - 1U) << 20) | ((bs1 - 1U) << 16) | (pre - 1U);
}
Complexity: O(1)

