Embedded C · STM32_HAL · volatile and race conditions · patterns · Advanced
Concept: Add to a shared value with a compare-and-swap loop that retries when another context changed it
Misconception: Reading a shared value, adding to it and writing it back is safe if the write is atomic.
Explanation: An atomic store alone does not help when the new value depends on the old one. The loop load, compute, compare-exchange succeeds only if the value is still the one the computation used; otherwise it reloads and tries again. On Cortex-M3 and above this compiles to load-exclusive and store-exclusive. Cortex-M0 has no exclusive instructions, so there the compiler needs a library or a short interrupt-off section.
Incorrect:
#include <stdatomic.h>
uint32_t addBad(_Atomic uint32_t *a, uint32_t n, void (*between)(void)) {
  uint32_t v = atomic_load(a);
  between();
  atomic_store(a, v + n);
