Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · clock tree · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Compute the system clock from the PLL settings and reject values outside the VCO limits
Misconception: The system clock is the oscillator frequency times N divided by P.
Explanation: On the STM32F4 the PLL first divides the oscillator by M to get the VCO input (1 to 2 MHz, 2 MHz preferred), multiplies by N to get the VCO output (192 to 432 MHz), and divides by P (2, 4, 6 or 8) to get the system clock. With an 8 MHz crystal, M = 8, N = 336 and P = 2 give 1 MHz, 336 MHz and 168 MHz. Leaving out the division by M gives a number eight times too high, and values outside the limits must be refused.
Incorrect:
uint32_t sysclkBad(uint32_t hse, uint32_t m, uint32_t n, uint32_t p) {
  return hse * n / p;
}
Correct:
uint32_t sysclk(uint32_t hse, uint32_t m, uint32_t n, uint32_t p) {
  if (m < 2 || m > 63 || n < 50 || n > 432) return 0;
  if (p != 2 && p != 4 && p != 6 && p != 8) return 0;
  uint32_t vin = hse / m;
  uint64_t vco = (uint64_t)vin * n;
  if (vin < 1000000U || vin > 2000000U || vco < 192000000ULL || vco > 432000000ULL) return 0;
  return (uint32_t)(vco / p);
}
Complexity: O(1)

