Embedded C · STM32_HAL · crc calculation · mental-model · Intermediate
Concept: Compute an MSB-first CRC-8 by testing the top bit before each shift
Misconception: A bitwise CRC tests the lowest bit and shifts right.
Explanation: A CRC is the remainder of the message polynomial divided by a generator polynomial. For the common MSB-first form, each byte is XORed into the top of the register, then for each of 8 steps the register is shifted left and the polynomial is XORed in when the bit shifted out was 1. CRC-8 with polynomial 0x07 and initial value 0 gives 0xF4 for the ASCII text 123456789. Testing the low bit and shifting right is the reflected variant and needs a different polynomial constant.
Incorrect:
uint8_t crc8Bad(const uint8_t *p, size_t n) {
  uint8_t crc = 0;
  while (n--) {
    crc ^= *p++;
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
      crc = (crc & 1) ? (uint8_t)((crc >> 1) ^ 0x07) : (uint8_t)(crc >> 1);
  }
  return crc;
}
Correct:
uint8_t crc8(const uint8_t *p, size_t n) {
  uint8_t crc = 0;
  while (n--) {
    crc ^= *p++;
    for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++)
      crc = (crc & 0x80) ? (uint8_t)((crc << 1) ^ 0x07) : (uint8_t)(crc << 1);
  }
  return crc;
}
Complexity: O(n)

