Concept: Create a two-complement sum checksum so that all bytes of the record add up to zero
Misconception: The checksum byte is the bitwise inverse of the sum.
Explanation: Intel HEX and many bootloaders use a two-complement checksum: the checksum byte is the value that makes the 8-bit sum of all bytes of the record, checksum included, equal to zero. That is (0 - sum) & 0xFF, which is the inverse plus one. Using only the inverse is off by one, and the receiver check sum == 0 always fails.
Incorrect:
uint8_t sumBad(const uint8_t *p, size_t n) {
  uint8_t s = 0;
  while (n--) s = (uint8_t)(s + *p++);
  return (uint8_t)~s;
}
Correct:
uint8_t sum2c(const uint8_t *p, size_t n) {
  uint8_t s = 0;
  while (n--) s = (uint8_t)(s + *p++);
  return (uint8_t)(0U - s);
}
Complexity: O(n)

