Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · nvic and exti interrupts · creation · Intermediate
Concept: Route a pin to an EXTI line through SYSCFG, with its clock on, then pick the edge and unmask it
Misconception: Setting the mask bit in EXTI is enough to get a pin interrupt.
Explanation: Each EXTI line n can listen to pin n of any one port; SYSCFG_EXTICR[n / 4] holds a 4-bit port number for each line (A = 0, B = 1, C = 2). The SYSCFG registers are in a peripheral that needs its clock enabled in RCC first. Then choose the edge with RTSR or FTSR and unmask the line in IMR, and enable the NVIC interrupt of that line.
Incorrect:
void extiBad(unsigned line) {
  EXTI->IMR |= 1U << line;
}
Correct:
void extiConfig(unsigned port, unsigned line, int falling) {
  RCC->APB2ENR |= RCC_APB2ENR_SYSCFGEN;
  unsigned idx = line >> 2, sh = (line & 3U) * 4U;
  SYSCFG->EXTICR[idx] = (SYSCFG->EXTICR[idx] & ~(0xFU << sh)) | ((uint32_t)port << sh);
  if (falling) EXTI->FTSR |= 1U << line; else EXTI->RTSR |= 1U << line;
  EXTI->IMR |= 1U << line;
}
Complexity: O(1)

