Embedded C · STM32_HAL · gpio registers · access · Intermediate
Concept: Select an alternate function through AFR[pin / 8] using four bits per pin
Misconception: One 32-bit AFR word holds the alternate function of all 16 pins.
Explanation: Each pin needs a 4-bit alternate-function number (AF0 to AF15), so a 32-bit register holds only 8 pins. The 16 pins are split over AFR[0] (pins 0 to 7) and AFR[1] (pins 8 to 15). The index is pin >> 3 and the shift is (pin & 7) * 4. Shifting by pin * 4 for pin 8 or higher shifts a 32-bit value by 32 or more, which is undefined behaviour in C. The pin must also be in alternate-function mode (MODER = 10).
Incorrect:
void gpioAFBad(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin, uint32_t af) {
  g->AFR[0] |= af << (pin * 4U);
}
Correct:
void gpioAF(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin, uint32_t af) {
  unsigned idx = pin >> 3, sh = (pin & 7U) * 4U;
  g->AFR[idx] = (g->AFR[idx] & ~(0xFU << sh)) | (af << sh);
}
Complexity: O(1)

