Embedded C · STM32_HAL · gpio registers · creation · Beginner
Concept: Set a pin mode with a two-bit field in MODER: clear the field, then write the mode
Misconception: MODER has one bit per pin, so setting bit n makes pin n an output.
Explanation: Each pin owns two bits in MODER: 00 input, 01 general-purpose output, 10 alternate function, 11 analog. Pin n sits at bit 2n. OR-ing 1 << n sets the wrong field (bit 5 belongs to pin 2) and can never clear an old mode. Clear the two bits with a mask, then OR in the new mode shifted to 2n. PUPDR and OSPEEDR use the same two-bit layout.
Incorrect:
void gpioModeBad(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin) {
  g->MODER |= 1U << pin;
}
Correct:
void gpioMode(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin, uint32_t mode) {
  g->MODER = (g->MODER & ~(3U << (pin * 2))) | (mode << (pin * 2));
}
Complexity: O(1)

