Embedded C · STM32_HAL · gpio registers · fit-and-alternatives · Intermediate
Concept: Choose open-drain with OTYPER for shared lines: the pin only pulls low, a resistor pulls high
Misconception: An open-drain output can drive the pin high.
Explanation: OTYPER bit n = 1 selects open-drain: writing 0 to ODR pulls the line to ground, writing 1 releases it and an external (or internal) pull-up brings it high. Several open-drain outputs on one wire form a wired-AND: the line is high only if every device releases it. I2C relies on this. Push-pull (bit = 0) drives both levels, so two push-pull outputs fighting on one wire short each other.
Incorrect:
void gpioSharedBad(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin) {
  g->OTYPER &= ~(1U << pin);
}
Correct:
void gpioOpenDrain(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin) {
  g->OTYPER |= 1U << pin;
}
int busLevel(unsigned releasedMask, unsigned devices) {
  return releasedMask == (1U << devices) - 1U;
}
Complexity: O(1)

