Embedded C · STM32_HAL · gpio registers · access · Beginner
Concept: Give an input a pull-up in PUPDR and read a pressed button as 0
Misconception: An unconnected input reads 0, and a pressed button reads 1.
Explanation: An input with no driver and no pull resistor floats and can read either level. PUPDR (two bits per pin: 00 none, 01 pull-up, 10 pull-down) ties the pin to a defined level through an internal resistor. A button wired to ground then reads 1 when idle and 0 when pressed, so the code must treat the pin as active low. The level is read from IDR, bit n.
Incorrect:
int buttonPressedBad(const GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin) {
  return (g->IDR >> pin) & 1U;
}
Correct:
void gpioPull(GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin, uint32_t pull) {
  g->PUPDR = (g->PUPDR & ~(3U << (pin * 2))) | (pull << (pin * 2));
}
int buttonPressed(const GPIO_TypeDef *g, unsigned pin) {
  return ((g->IDR >> pin) & 1U) == 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

