Embedded C · STM32_HAL · h-bridge motor driver · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Turn a signed speed into the two input duties of an H-bridge channel
Misconception: A negative speed can be written straight to the PWM compare register.
Explanation: A dual-input driver such as the DRV8833 turns forward when only IN1 is driven and reverse when only IN2 is driven. A signed speed therefore becomes: positive gives IN1 = duty and IN2 = 0, negative gives IN1 = 0 and IN2 = duty, zero gives both low (coast). The duty is the absolute value, limited to the PWM range. Writing a negative number to an unsigned compare register wraps it to a large positive duty.
Incorrect:
void hbBad(int speed, uint8_t *in1, uint8_t *in2) {
  *in1 = (uint8_t)speed;
  *in2 = 0;
}
Correct:
void hbDrive(int speed, uint8_t *in1, uint8_t *in2) {
  int mag = speed < 0 ? -speed : speed;
  if (mag > 255) mag = 255;
  *in1 = speed > 0 ? (uint8_t)mag : 0;
  *in2 = speed < 0 ? (uint8_t)mag : 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

