Embedded C · AVR_Bare_Metal · non-blocking isr design · mutation · Beginner
Concept: Take an ISR event flag with an atomic test-and-clear, and clear it before processing
Misconception: Clearing the flag after processing the event is the same as clearing it before.
Explanation: If the ISR sets the flag again while the main loop is still processing the previous event, clearing the flag afterwards erases the new event. Read and clear the flag in one step with interrupts off, then process; a new event that arrives during processing sets the flag again and is handled on the next pass. The flag must be volatile because the compiler cannot see the interrupt write it.
Incorrect:
volatile uint8_t evBad;
uint8_t pollBad(void (*process)(void)) {
  if (!evBad) return 0;
  process();
  evBad = 0;
  return 1;
