Concept: Count dropped bytes in the receive ISR instead of waiting for space
Misconception: If the buffer is full the ISR should wait until the main loop makes room.
Explanation: An ISR that waits for the main loop to free a slot never returns, because the main loop cannot run while the ISR is executing. The ISR must finish quickly: when the buffer is full it drops the byte and increments a counter. The main code can report or react to the counter. Ignoring the return value of the push loses data without any sign.
Incorrect:
void rxIsrBad(Ring *r, uint8_t b) {
  ringPush(r, b);
}
Correct:
Ring rxRing;
volatile uint8_t dropped;
ISR(USART_RX_vect) {
  if (!ringPush(&rxRing, UDR0)) dropped++;
}
Complexity: O(1)

