Embedded C · ESP_IDF · lwip networking · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Compute the RFC 1071 Internet checksum with end-around carry and an odd trailing byte
Misconception: Summing the 16-bit words in a 16-bit variable gives the checksum.
Explanation: The Internet checksum, used by IPv4, ICMP, UDP and TCP, is the one's complement of the one's complement sum of the data taken as 16-bit big-endian words. Carries out of bit 15 must be added back (end-around carry), so accumulate in 32 bits and fold. A trailing odd byte is padded with a zero byte on the right. The RFC 1071 example bytes 00 01 F2 03 F4 F5 F6 F7 give 220D; a 16-bit accumulator drops the carry and gives 220F. A packet that includes its correct checksum sums to zero.
Incorrect:
uint16_t inetBad(const uint8_t *d, size_t n) {
  uint16_t s = 0;
  while (n > 1) { s = (uint16_t)(s + (d[0] << 8 | d[1])); d += 2; n -= 2; }
  return (uint16_t)~s;
}
Correct:
uint16_t inetSum(const uint8_t *d, size_t n) {
  uint32_t s = 0;
  while (n > 1) { s += (uint32_t)(d[0] << 8 | d[1]); d += 2; n -= 2; }
  if (n) s += (uint32_t)d[0] << 8;
  while (s >> 16) s = (s & 0xFFFFU) + (s >> 16);
  return (uint16_t)~s;
}
Complexity: O(n)

