Embedded C · STM32_HAL · memory mapped io · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Compute the bit-band alias address of a peripheral bit on Cortex-M3 and Cortex-M4
Misconception: Bit-banding is available on every Cortex-M core.
Explanation: Cortex-M3 and Cortex-M4 map each bit of the peripheral region 0x40000000 and the SRAM region 0x20000000 to one word in an alias region, so that a single store sets or clears one bit atomically. The alias address is alias_base + (byte_offset x 32) + (bit x 4). Cortex-M0, M0+ (which includes the RP2040) and M7 have no bit-band region, so portable code uses the set and clear registers of the peripheral instead.
Incorrect:
uint32_t bitBandBad(uint32_t addr, unsigned bit) {
  return 0x42000000U + (addr - 0x40000000U) * 4U + bit * 4U;
}
Correct:
uint32_t bitBand(uint32_t addr, unsigned bit) {
  return 0x42000000U + (addr - 0x40000000U) * 32U + bit * 4U;
}
Complexity: O(1)

