Embedded C · STM32_HAL · memory mapped io · creation · Intermediate
Concept: Describe a peripheral as a struct of volatile registers and check offsets at compile time
Misconception: A struct overlay needs members only for the registers the driver uses.
Explanation: A struct laid over the peripheral base address works only if every register sits at the right offset, so unused and reserved registers must still be represented. Leaving out a reserved gap moves every later register. Assert the offsets with _Static_assert and offsetof so a mistake stops the build. Use volatile uint32_t members and make the base pointer constant.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { volatile uint32_t SR, DR, CR1; } DevBad;
size_t crOffBad(void) { return offsetof(DevBad, CR1); }
Correct:
typedef struct {
  volatile uint32_t SR, DR;
  uint32_t RESERVED0[2];
  volatile uint32_t CR1;
} Dev;
_Static_assert(offsetof(Dev, CR1) == 0x10, "CR1 must be at 0x10");
size_t crOff(void) { return offsetof(Dev, CR1); }
Complexity: O(1)

