Embedded C · STM32_HAL · memory mapped io · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Poll a status register with a fresh read on every pass and a bounded number of tries
Misconception: Reading the status register once before the loop is enough.
Explanation: A wait loop must read the register again on each pass, otherwise it tests a stale copy and never sees the flag change. It should also give up after a bounded number of tries and return an error, so a missing clock or a broken peripheral does not hang the program.
Incorrect:
int waitBad(uint32_t (*rd)(void), uint32_t mask, int tries) {
  uint32_t s = rd();
  for (int i = 0; i < tries; i++)
    if (s & mask) return 0;
  return -1;
}
Correct:
int waitSet(uint32_t (*rd)(void), uint32_t mask, int tries) {
  for (int i = 0; i < tries; i++)
    if (rd() & mask) return 0;
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(tries)

