Concept: Decide when to send PINGREQ with an elapsed-time test that survives the millisecond counter wrapping
Misconception: Comparing the current time with the last send time plus the interval works for any counter value.
Explanation: The broker drops a client that is silent for 1.5 times the keep-alive, so the client sends a PINGREQ after half the interval of silence (keep-alive in seconds x 500 milliseconds). The test must use the unsigned difference now - last. Comparing now >= last + interval fails near the wrap of the 32-bit counter: last + interval overflows to a small number and the ping is sent far too early.
Incorrect:
int pingBad(uint32_t now, uint32_t last, uint32_t keepSec) {
  return now >= last + keepSec * 500U;
}
Correct:
int pingDue(uint32_t now, uint32_t last, uint32_t keepSec) {
  return (uint32_t)(now - last) >= keepSec * 500U;
}
Complexity: O(1)

