Embedded C · STM32_HAL · nvic and exti interrupts · access · Beginner
Concept: Read the enable state of an interrupt from ISER[n / 32] bit n % 32
Misconception: ISER[0] holds the enable bit of every interrupt.
Explanation: The NVIC spreads its enable bits over several 32-bit words: interrupt n lives in ISER[n >> 5] at bit n & 31. USART2 is interrupt 38, so it is bit 6 of ISER[1]. Shifting ISER[0] right by 38 is undefined behaviour in C. ISER is write-1-to-set and reads back the current enable state; to enable an interrupt use NVIC_EnableIRQ, which writes only the right bit.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: shift exponent):
int irqEnabledBad(uint32_t n) {
  return (int)((NVIC->ISER[0] >> n) & 1U);
}
Correct:
int irqEnabled(uint32_t n) {
  return (int)((NVIC->ISER[n >> 5] >> (n & 31U)) & 1U);
}
Complexity: O(1)

