Embedded C · STM32_HAL · pointers · mental-model · Beginner
Concept: Advance a pointer by a number of bytes by casting to a byte pointer first
Misconception: Adding n to a pointer moves it n bytes.
Explanation: Pointer arithmetic is scaled by the size of the pointed-to type: adding 1 to a uint32_t pointer moves 4 bytes. To move by bytes, cast to a uint8_t pointer, add, and cast back. Forgetting the scale is the usual cause of reading the wrong word of a buffer or register block.
Incorrect:
const uint32_t *skipBad(const uint32_t *p, size_t nBytes) {
  return p + nBytes;
}
Correct:
const uint32_t *skipBytes(const uint32_t *p, size_t nBytes) {
  return (const uint32_t *)((const uint8_t *)p + nBytes);
}
Complexity: O(1)

