Embedded C · STM32_HAL · pointers · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Check pointer arguments for NULL and return an error code instead of dereferencing
Misconception: A pointer passed by a caller is always valid.
Explanation: A function that receives a pointer from code it does not control should check it before use. On a Cortex-M core address 0 is the start of flash or the vector table and reading it does not fault, so a NULL dereference can silently return garbage instead of crashing. Return an error code and write the result through an output pointer.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: null pointer):
int readBad(const uint16_t *p) {
  return *p;
}
Correct:
int readU16(const uint16_t *p, uint16_t *out) {
  if (p == NULL || out == NULL) return -1;
  *out = *p;
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

