Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · reset and brown-out · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Pick the newer of two stored records so a power loss during a write cannot destroy the settings
Misconception: Writing settings to one place in flash is enough.
Explanation: A power failure in the middle of a write can leave a record half-written. Keep two copies, write the older one, and mark each with a sequence number and a check value. At start-up take the valid record with the newer sequence. Compare sequence numbers by subtracting them as unsigned values so the choice stays right after the counter wraps.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { uint32_t seq; uint32_t data; uint32_t sum; } Rec;
int validBad(const Rec *r) { return r->seq != 0xFFFFFFFFU; }
int newerBad(const Rec *a, const Rec *b) { return a->seq > b->seq ? 0 : 1; }
Correct:
typedef struct { uint32_t seq; uint32_t data; uint32_t sum; } Rec;
int recValid(const Rec *r) { return r->sum == (r->seq ^ r->data ^ 0xA5A5A5A5U); }
int pickRec(const Rec *a, const Rec *b) {
  int va = recValid(a), vb = recValid(b);
  if (va && vb) return (int32_t)(a->seq - b->seq) > 0 ? 0 : 1;
  if (va) return 0;
  if (vb) return 1;
  return -1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

