Embedded C · ESP_IDF · rtos semaphores and mutexes · mental-model · Intermediate
Concept: Use a counting semaphore when several events can arrive before the task runs
Misconception: A binary semaphore remembers every event given to it.
Explanation: A binary semaphore holds at most one token. If an interrupt gives it three times before the task runs, the task takes it once and two events are lost. A counting semaphore keeps the count, so the task takes it three times. Use a binary semaphore for a flag-like signal and a counting semaphore when each event must be handled.
Incorrect:
int handledBad(int gives) {
  SemaphoreHandle_t s = xSemaphoreCreateBinary();
  for (int i = 0; i < gives; i++) xSemaphoreGive(s);
  int n = 0;
  while (xSemaphoreTake(s, 0) == pdTRUE) n++;
  vSemaphoreDelete(s);
  return n;
}
Correct:
int handledEvents(int gives) {
  SemaphoreHandle_t s = xSemaphoreCreateCounting(16, 0);
  for (int i = 0; i < gives; i++) xSemaphoreGive(s);
  int n = 0;
  while (xSemaphoreTake(s, 0) == pdTRUE) n++;
  vSemaphoreDelete(s);
  return n;
}
Complexity: O(n)

