Embedded C · STM32_HAL · static allocation · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Measure stack high-water mark by painting the stack with a pattern and scanning from the far end
Misconception: The stack grows from the low end of its region, so scan from the high end.
Explanation: On Cortex-M, AVR and Xtensa the stack grows toward lower addresses. Fill the whole stack region with a known pattern such as 0xA5 at start-up. Later, scan from the low address upward until the first byte that is not the pattern: everything below that was never touched. The used depth is the region size minus the untouched bytes. Scanning from the wrong end reports the used part as unused.
Incorrect:
size_t paintBad(const uint8_t *s, size_t n) {
  size_t k = 0;
  while (k < n && s[n - 1 - k] == 0xA5) k++;
  return n - k;
}
Correct:
size_t paintUsed(const uint8_t *s, size_t n) {
  size_t k = 0;
  while (k < n && s[k] == 0xA5) k++;
  return n - k;
}
Complexity: O(n)

