Embedded C · STM32_HAL · superloop and scheduler · iteration · Intermediate
Concept: Run due tasks from the superloop and advance each task time by its period to avoid drift
Misconception: After running a task, store the current time as its last run time.
Explanation: If the scheduler records now as the last run time, every delay in noticing that the task was due is added to all following periods and the task drifts later and later. Advance last by the period instead, which keeps the average rate exact. Tasks must run to completion and never wait, because one blocking task delays every other.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { void (*fn)(void); uint32_t period, last; } Task;
int runBad(Task *t, int n, uint32_t now) {
  int ran = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if ((uint32_t)(now - t[i].last) >= t[i].period) {
      t[i].fn(); t[i].last = now; ran++;
    }
  return ran;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { void (*fn)(void); uint32_t period, last; } Task;
int schedRun(Task *t, int n, uint32_t now) {
  int ran = 0;
  for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if ((uint32_t)(now - t[i].last) >= t[i].period) {
      t[i].fn(); t[i].last += t[i].period; ran++;
    }
  return ran;
}
Complexity: O(n)

