Embedded C · STM32_HAL · stack and heap · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Set a pointer to NULL after freeing it so a later use is detectable
Misconception: After free the pointer can still be used to read the old contents.
Explanation: free releases the block but does not change the pointer, so the pointer now dangles and any use is undefined behaviour: it may appear to work for months until the block is reused. Passing the address of the pointer to a helper that frees it and sets it to NULL turns a later bug into an obvious NULL check.
Incorrect (undefined behaviour reported by the sanitizer: heap-use-after-free):
void releaseBad(void **pp) {
  free(*pp);
}
Correct:
void release(void **pp) {
  free(*pp);
  *pp = NULL;
}
Complexity: O(1)

