Embedded C · STM32_HAL · spi master · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Keep chip select low for the whole transaction and set the read bit in the register address
Misconception: Toggling chip select around every byte is the safe way to talk to a device.
Explanation: Many SPI devices treat the falling edge of chip select as the start of a command and the rising edge as the end. In a register read the first byte is the register address (with a read flag, commonly bit 7 set) and the following bytes are the data, so releasing chip select between bytes aborts the command and the device never returns data. Pull chip select low once, clock out the address, clock in n bytes, and release it.
Incorrect:
typedef struct { void (*cs)(int); uint8_t (*xfer)(uint8_t); } SpiDev;
int regReadBad(const SpiDev *d, uint8_t reg, uint8_t *buf, unsigned n) {
  d->cs(0); d->xfer(reg | 0x80); d->cs(1);
  for (unsigned i = 0; i < n; i++) { d->cs(0); buf[i] = d->xfer(0xFF); d->cs(1); }
  return (int)n;
}
Correct:
typedef struct { void (*cs)(int); uint8_t (*xfer)(uint8_t); } SpiDev;
int spiRegRead(const SpiDev *d, uint8_t reg, uint8_t *buf, unsigned n) {
  d->cs(0);
  d->xfer(reg | 0x80);
  for (unsigned i = 0; i < n; i++) buf[i] = d->xfer(0xFF);
  d->cs(1);
  return (int)n;
}
Complexity: O(n)

