Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · structs and unions · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Write a 16-bit value in network byte order with shifts so it does not depend on the CPU
Misconception: Copying the bytes of a uint16_t with memcpy gives network byte order.
Explanation: Network protocols and many device registers use big-endian order, with the high byte first. ARM Cortex-M, AVR and ESP32 cores store values little-endian, so memcpy of a uint16_t puts the low byte first. Writing (v >> 8) and then v with shifts gives the same bytes on every CPU.
Incorrect:
void putBe16Bad(uint8_t *p, uint16_t v) {
  memcpy(p, &v, 2);
}
Correct:
void putBe16(uint8_t *p, uint16_t v) {
  p[0] = (uint8_t)(v >> 8);
  p[1] = (uint8_t)v;
}
uint16_t getBe16(const uint8_t *p) {
  return (uint16_t)((p[0] << 8) | p[1]);
}
Complexity: O(1)

