Embedded C · STM32_HAL · servo and stepper motors · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Convert microseconds to timer counts with a 64-bit intermediate
Misconception: Multiplying microseconds by the timer frequency fits in 32 bits.
Explanation: Counts = microseconds x timer frequency / 1,000,000. With a 84 MHz timer clock and a pulse of 1500 microseconds the product is 126 billion, far above the 32-bit limit, so the unsigned 32-bit result wraps and the pulse is wrong. Use a 64-bit intermediate, or prescale the timer to 1 MHz so one count is one microsecond.
Incorrect:
uint32_t ccrBad(uint32_t us, uint32_t hz) {
  return us * hz / 1000000U;
}
Correct:
uint32_t usToCcr(uint32_t us, uint32_t hz) {
  return (uint32_t)((uint64_t)us * hz / 1000000U);
}
Complexity: O(1)

