Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · hardware timers · patterns · Intermediate
Concept: Turn input-capture ticks into microseconds with a 64-bit intermediate
Misconception: ticks * (PSC + 1) * 1000000 / clock fits in 32 bits.
Explanation: Input capture stores the counter value at an edge; the pulse width is the difference of two captures in ticks. To get microseconds multiply by the tick length first and divide by the clock last. The product ticks * (PSC + 1) * 1000000 reaches 3.4e12 for 40000 ticks at PSC 83, far beyond 32 bits, so use uint64_t for the product.
Incorrect:
uint32_t usBad(uint16_t ticks, uint32_t psc, uint32_t clk) {
  return ticks * (psc + 1U) * 1000000U / clk;
}
Correct:
uint32_t ticksToUs(uint16_t ticks, uint32_t psc, uint32_t clk) {
  return (uint32_t)((uint64_t)ticks * (psc + 1U) * 1000000U / clk);
}
uint32_t pulseUs(uint16_t rise, uint16_t fall, uint32_t psc, uint32_t clk) {
  return ticksToUs((uint16_t)(fall - rise), psc, clk);
}
Complexity: O(1)

