Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · uart and usart · complexity · Intermediate
Concept: Check the real baud rate error in parts per million before trusting a divider
Misconception: Any divider that is close enough works because UART has no clock line.
Explanation: Both ends sample each bit on their own clock, and the cumulative drift over a 10-bit frame must stay well under half a bit; a total mismatch of about 2 percent (20000 ppm) is a common limit and less is better. The actual rate is clock / BRR; compute the signed error in ppm in 64 bits so the product does not overflow, and reject dividers outside a budget.
Incorrect:
int32_t errBad(uint32_t pclk, uint32_t baud) {
  return (int32_t)(pclk / (pclk / baud) - baud);
}
Correct:
int32_t uartErrPpm(uint32_t pclk, uint32_t brr, uint32_t baud) {
  int64_t actual = (int64_t)pclk / (int64_t)brr;
  return (int32_t)((actual - (int64_t)baud) * 1000000 / (int64_t)baud);
}
int uartBaudOk(uint32_t pclk, uint32_t baud, int32_t maxPpm) {
  int32_t e = uartErrPpm(pclk, uartBrr(pclk, baud), baud);
  return (e < 0 ? -e : e) <= maxPpm;
}
Complexity: O(1)

