Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: STM32_HAL
Embedded C · STM32_HAL · uart and usart · edge-cases · Intermediate
Concept: Check the overrun, noise, framing and parity flags before using a received byte
Misconception: When RXNE is set, DR holds a valid byte.
Explanation: RXNE says a byte is waiting, but the same status register can also report that the byte had a parity error (PE), a framing error (FE, a bad stop bit, often a wrong baud rate), noise (NE) or that an earlier byte was overrun (ORE). Read SR once, test the error bits, then read DR (reading SR then DR is what clears the flags) and drop or count the byte if any error bit was set.
Incorrect:
int getBad(uint8_t *out) {
  if (!(USART2->SR & USART_SR_RXNE)) return 0;
  *out = (uint8_t)USART2->DR;
  return 1;
}
Correct:
int uartGetc(uint8_t *out, uint32_t *errors) {
  uint32_t sr = USART2->SR;
  if (!(sr & (USART_SR_RXNE | USART_SR_ORE))) return 0;
  uint8_t b = (uint8_t)USART2->DR;
  if (sr & (USART_SR_ORE | USART_SR_NE | USART_SR_FE | USART_SR_PE)) {
    (*errors)++;
    return -1;
  }
  *out = b;
  return 1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

