Embedded C · STM32_HAL · watchdog timers · transformation · Beginner
Concept: Choose the smallest IWDG prescaler whose reload value still fits in 12 bits
Misconception: The reload register can hold any count, so the prescaler never matters.
Explanation: The independent watchdog counts down from RLR at LSI / divider, with a 12-bit reload (at most 4095) and a prescaler of 4, 8, ... 256. The timeout is (RLR + 1) * divider / f_LSI. The LSI is an uncalibrated RC oscillator, nominally 32 kHz on many STM32F4 parts but with a wide tolerance, so the real timeout varies and the margin must be generous. Pick the smallest divider that fits, for the best resolution, and report failure when even /256 is not enough.
Incorrect:
uint32_t rlrBad(uint32_t ms, uint32_t lsi, uint32_t div) {
  return (uint32_t)((uint64_t)ms * lsi / (1000U * div));
}
Correct:
int iwdgCalc(uint32_t ms, uint32_t lsi, uint32_t *pr, uint32_t *rlr) {
  for (uint32_t code = 0, div = 4; code <= 6; code++, div *= 2U) {
    uint64_t ticks = (uint64_t)ms * lsi / (1000U * div);
    if (ticks >= 1 && ticks - 1 <= 4095) {
      *pr = code;
      *rlr = (uint32_t)(ticks - 1);
      return 1;
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(1)

