Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: c; platform: ESP_IDF
Embedded C · ESP_IDF · wifi station · transformation · Intermediate
Concept: Compute a reconnect delay that doubles on every failure and stops at a cap
Misconception: Shifting the base delay left by the attempt number gives the delay.
Explanation: Doubling is a left shift by one per failed attempt, but without a cap the delay grows without bound, and after about 32 doublings the shift is undefined. Double until the cap is reached and then stay there: a 500 ms base with an 8000 ms cap gives 500, 1000, 2000, 4000, 8000, 8000. A capped delay keeps a fleet of devices from reconnecting in lockstep after an access point restarts; adding a random offset spreads them further.
Incorrect:
uint32_t backoffBad(uint32_t base, unsigned n, uint32_t cap) {
  (void)cap;
  return base << n;
}
Correct:
uint32_t backoffMs(uint32_t base, unsigned n, uint32_t cap) {
  uint32_t d = base;
  while (n-- > 0 && d < cap) d <<= 1;
  return d < cap ? d : cap;
}
Complexity: O(n)

