Embedded C · STM32_HAL · ws2812 led strips · transformation · Advanced
Concept: Derive the timer period and the compare values for the 800 kHz bit stream from the timer clock
Misconception: The period register equals the timer clock divided by 800 kHz.
Explanation: A bit takes 1.25 microseconds. A timer counts from 0 to ARR, so the period is ARR + 1 ticks and ARR = clock / 800000 - 1; at 84 MHz that is 104. A zero bit is a high pulse of about 0.4 microseconds and a one bit about 0.8 microseconds, so the compare values are clock x time rounded to the nearest tick: 34 and 67. Truncating or forgetting the minus one moves the pulses by a tick, which is a large part of the tolerance.
Incorrect:
uint32_t arrBad(uint32_t clk) { return clk / 800000U; }
uint32_t ccrTrunc(uint32_t clk, uint32_t ns) {
    return (uint32_t)((uint64_t)clk * ns / 1000000000ULL);
}
Correct:
uint32_t wsArr(uint32_t clk) { return clk / 800000U - 1U; }
uint32_t wsCcr(uint32_t clk, uint32_t ns) {
  return (uint32_t)(((uint64_t)clk * ns + 500000000ULL) / 1000000000ULL);
}
Complexity: O(1)

