Python (embedded) · signal filtering and PID control for microcontrollers · iteration · L1
Concept: A moving average keeps a fixed window and divides by the window size
Misconception: Averaging every reading since start-up smooths the signal best.
Explanation: An average over all readings reacts more and more slowly and its sum grows without limit. A moving average keeps only the last n values. Add the new value, drop the oldest when the window is full and divide by the number of values held. Dividing by the window size before it fills gives values that are too low.
Incorrect:
class AvgBad:
    def __init__(self):
        self.vals = []
    def add(self, v):
        self.vals.append(v)
        return sum(self.vals) // len(self.vals)
Correct:
class MovingAvg:
    def __init__(self, n):
        self.n = n
        self.vals = []
    def add(self, v):
        self.vals.append(v)
        if len(self.vals) > self.n:
            self.vals.pop(0)
        return sum(self.vals) // len(self.vals)
Complexity: O(n)

