Python (embedded) · I2C on MicroPython boards · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Retry an I2C transaction a few times before giving up, because OSError can be transient
Misconception: An OSError from I2C always means the device is missing.
Explanation: A busy or noisy bus can raise OSError (ENODEV or ETIMEDOUT) for one transaction and work on the next. Retry a small fixed number of times, wait briefly between tries and re-raise the last error so a real fault is not hidden. Never loop forever.
Incorrect:
def read_bad(dev):
    return dev.read()
Correct:
import time
def read_retry(dev, tries=3):
    for i in range(tries):
        try:
            return dev.read()
        except OSError:
            if i == tries - 1:
                raise
            time.sleep_ms(5)
Complexity: O(tries)

