Python (embedded) · UART serial on MicroPython boards · creation · L3
Concept: Drain the receive buffer in a bounded loop so a chatty device cannot starve the program
Misconception: One read(4) empties the buffer.
Explanation: The buffer may hold more than you asked for, and the data keeps arriving. Loop while any() is positive, collect the bytes and stop at a limit so the loop ends even if the sender never stops. Anything left over stays in the driver buffer for the next pass.
Incorrect:
def drain_bad(uart):
    return uart.read(4)
Correct:
def drain(uart, limit=64):
    out = bytearray()
    while uart.any() and len(out) < limit:
        out.extend(uart.read(min(uart.any(), limit - len(out))))
    return bytes(out)
Complexity: O(n)

