Python (embedded) · UART serial on MicroPython boards · iteration · L2
Concept: Accumulate received bytes in a buffer and split complete lines, since a read can end mid-line
Misconception: Each read() returns one complete line.
Explanation: Bytes arrive in arbitrary chunks, so one read may end in the middle of a line or contain two lines. Append every chunk to a bytearray, split off complete lines at the newline byte, and keep the remainder for the next call. Handle both b"\r\n" and b"\n" endings by stripping the line.
Incorrect:
def lines_bad(chunk):
    return chunk.split(b"\n")
Correct:
class LineBuffer:
    def __init__(self):
        self.buf = bytearray()
    def feed(self, chunk):
        self.buf.extend(chunk)
        out = []
        while True:
            i = self.buf.find(b"\n")
            if i < 0:
                return out
            out.append(bytes(self.buf[:i]).strip())
            self.buf = self.buf[i + 1:]
Complexity: O(n)

