Python · searching · access · L1
Concept: bisect_left returns an insertion point, not a found flag; check the element at that position
Misconception: bisect.bisect_left(a, x) tells whether x is in the list.
Explanation: bisect_left returns the leftmost position where x could be inserted and keep the list sorted. That position can be len(a), or the index of a larger value, when x is missing. Test membership by checking that the index is in range and a[i] == x. bisect_right returns the position after any equal values, so right - left counts the copies, and insort inserts at the bisect_right position.
Incorrect:
import bisect
def has_bad(a, x):
    return bisect.bisect_left(a, x) < len(a)
Correct:
import bisect
def has(a, x):
    i = bisect.bisect_left(a, x)
    return i < len(a) and a[i] == x
def count(a, x):
    return bisect.bisect_right(a, x) - bisect.bisect_left(a, x)
Complexity: O(log n)

