Python · recursion_dp · edge-cases · L1
Concept: functools.cache needs hashable arguments; pass a tuple or an index instead of a list
Misconception: functools.cache can memoize a function that takes a list.
Explanation: cache stores results in a dict keyed by the arguments, so every argument must be hashable. Passing a list raises TypeError: unhashable type. Convert to a tuple at the boundary, or pass an index into data kept outside the function. A tuple of mutable items is still unhashable, and mutating data that a cached function reads makes cached answers stale.
Incorrect:
from functools import cache

@cache
def total_bad(nums):
    return sum(nums)
Correct:
from functools import cache

def total(nums):
    nums = tuple(nums)
    @cache
    def go(i):
        return 0 if i == len(nums) else nums[i] + go(i + 1)
    return go(0)
Complexity: O(n)

