Python · graph_traversal · patterns · L2
Concept: In an undirected graph a visited neighbour means a cycle only if it is not the node you came from
Misconception: If DFS reaches an already visited neighbour, the graph has a cycle.
Explanation: In an undirected graph every edge is stored in both directions, so from a child the parent is always a visited neighbour. Treating that as a cycle reports a cycle in every tree. Pass the parent along and ignore it; any other visited neighbour closes a real cycle. (With parallel edges between the same two nodes compare edge ids instead of nodes.)
Incorrect:
def has_cycle_bad(adj):
    seen = set()
    for s in range(len(adj)):
        if s in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(s)
        stack = [s]
        while stack:
            u = stack.pop()
            for v in adj[u]:
                if v in seen:
                    return True
                seen.add(v)
                stack.append(v)
    return False
Correct:
def has_cycle(adj):
    seen = set()
    for s in range(len(adj)):
        if s in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(s)
        stack = [(s, -1)]
        while stack:
            u, parent = stack.pop()
            for v in adj[u]:
                if v == parent:
                    continue
                if v in seen:
                    return True
                seen.add(v)
                stack.append((v, u))
    return False
Complexity: O(V + E)

