Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: ruby
Ruby · Bloom filters · insertion-and-deletion · L3
Concept: A Bloom filter cannot delete; clearing bits breaks other items, so use counters
Misconception: Removing an item means clearing its k bits.
Explanation: Those bits may also belong to other items, which would then be reported absent: false negatives, which a Bloom filter must never produce. A counting Bloom filter keeps a small counter per position, increments on add and decrements on remove, and treats a position as set when its counter is above zero. It uses several times the memory. Only remove items that were added.
Incorrect:
class BloomFilter
  def remove_bad(item)
    positions(item).each { |i| @bits &= ~(1 << i) }
    self
  end
end
Correct:
class CountingBloom < BloomFilter
  def initialize(m, k)
    super
    @counts = Array.new(m, 0)
  end
  def add(item)
    positions(item).each { |i| @counts[i] += 1 }
    self
  end
  def include?(item)
    positions(item).all? { |i| @counts[i] > 0 }
  end
  def remove(item)
    return false unless include?(item)
    positions(item).each { |i| @counts[i] -= 1 }
    true
  end
end
Complexity: O(k)

