Ruby · doubly linked lists · patterns · L3
Concept: An LRU cache pairs a Hash for lookup with a doubly linked list for recency order
Misconception: A Hash alone can evict the least recently used key, even if get never refreshes it.
Explanation: A Hash keeps insertion order, not usage order, so get must move the entry to the most recent end. A list gives O(1) unlink and relink, and the Hash maps each key to its node, so get and put are both O(1). Evict the node before the tail sentinel and delete its key from the Hash as well.
Incorrect:
class LRUBad
  def initialize(cap)
    @cap = cap
    @h = {}
  end
  def get(k)
    @h[k]
  end
  def put(k, v)
    @h.delete(k)
    @h[k] = v
    @h.shift if @h.size > @cap
  end
end
Correct:
class LRU
  def initialize(cap)
    @cap = cap
    @map = {}
    @list = DList.new
  end
  def get(k)
    node = @map[k]
    return nil unless node
    @list.remove(node)
    @list.link_after(@list.head, node)
    node.value[1]
  end
  def put(k, v)
    if (node = @map[k])
      @list.remove(node)
      @list.link_after(@list.head, node)
      node.value = [k, v]
      return v
    end
    if @map.size >= @cap
      old = @list.tail.prev
      @list.remove(old)
      @map.delete(old.value[0])
    end
    @map[k] = @list.push_front([k, v])
    v
  end
end
Complexity: O(1)

