Ruby · priority queues · ordering · L2
Concept: For top-k with ties, put the tie-break in the comparator as well as the main key
Misconception: Ordering by count alone is enough to get the k most frequent words.
Explanation: Words with equal counts then come out in whatever order the heap happens to produce, so results are not reproducible or alphabetical. Compare counts descending first and, when equal, the words ascending. tally builds the counts in O(n) and the queue pops k times at O(log m) each.
Incorrect:
def top_k_bad(words, k)
  counts = words.tally
  q = PQ.new { |a, b| b[1] <=> a[1] }
  counts.each { |w, n| q.push([w, n]) }
  Array.new([k, counts.size].min) { q.pop[0] }
end
Correct:
def top_k_words(words, k)
  counts = words.tally
  q = PQ.new do |a, b|
    c = b[1] <=> a[1]
    c.zero? ? a[0] <=> b[0] : c
  end
  counts.each { |w, n| q.push([w, n]) }
  Array.new([k, counts.size].min) { q.pop[0] }
end
Complexity: O(n + k log m)

