Ruby · queues · patterns · L3
Concept: A queue from two stacks moves items across only when the output stack is empty
Misconception: dequeue can always move everything from the input stack to the output stack first.
Explanation: Moving every time puts newer items on top of older ones that are still waiting in the output stack, so the order is wrong. Transfer only when the output stack is empty. Each element moves at most once, so dequeue is O(1) amortised.
Incorrect:
class TwoStackBad
  def initialize
    @in = []
    @out = []
  end
  def enqueue(v)
    @in.push(v)
    self
  end
  def dequeue
    @out.push(@in.pop) until @in.empty?
    @out.pop
  end
end
Correct:
class TwoStackQueue
  def initialize
    @in = []
    @out = []
  end
  def enqueue(v)
    @in.push(v)
    self
  end
  def dequeue
    @out.push(@in.pop) until @in.empty? if @out.empty?
    @out.pop
  end
  def size
    @in.size + @out.size
  end
end
Complexity: O(1)

