Ruby · stacks · patterns · L3
Concept: A min-stack keeps a second stack of running minimums so pop can restore the previous minimum
Misconception: A single @min variable updated on every push is enough to answer min after pops.
Explanation: When the current minimum is popped the single variable is stale and the previous minimum is lost. Store with each value the minimum at that depth, or keep a parallel stack of minimums. Then push, pop and min are all O(1), at the cost of O(n) extra space.
Incorrect:
class MinStackBad
  def initialize
    @items = []
    @min = nil
  end
  def push(v)
    @items.push(v)
    @min = @min ? [@min, v].min : v
  end
  def pop
    @items.pop
  end
  def min
    @min
  end
end
Correct:
class MinStack
  def initialize
    @items = []
    @mins = []
  end
  def push(v)
    @items.push(v)
    @mins.push(@mins.empty? ? v : [@mins.last, v].min)
    self
  end
  def pop
    @mins.pop
    @items.pop
  end
  def min
    @mins.last
  end
end
Complexity: O(1)

