2. Overview and mental model: Weighted Graph
A weighted graph attaches a cost to every edge. The adjacency list stores, for each vertex, an array of [neighbour,
weight] tuples.
Shortest paths use Dijkstra (non-negative weights), Bellman-Ford (negative weights, detects negative cycles) or
Floyd-Warshall (all pairs). Minimum spanning trees use Prim or Kruskal.
Use weighted graphs for roads, networks, costs and time. The examples use O(V squared) array scans instead of a
heap to stay short; a binary heap lowers Dijkstra and Prim to O(E log V).
Toolbox
 const g = new WGraph(3);
g.addEdge(0, 1, 4); g.addEdge(0, 2, 1); g.addEdge(2, 1, 1);
dijkstra(g, 0);       // [0, 2, 1]
3. Core concepts (tested chunks)
2 concept chunks, ordered by level. Read the misconception first and try to predict why it fails before reading the
explanation.

TypeScript Weighted Graph: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 3
Type each edge as a [to, weight] tuple so the compiler rejects an edge without a weight
ts-weightedgraph-edge-type   lens: creation   level: L1   family: ts-wg-creation
Prerequisites: none. Follow-up: ts-weightedgraph-undirected. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. An edge can be pushed as [v] and the weight added later.
A weighted adjacency list stores pairs. Typing the pair as a tuple [to: number, w: number] makes the compiler reject a
push of [v] with TS2345, which would otherwise create an edge with an undefined weight and produce NaN distances
later. Array.from builds one list per vertex.
Incorrect (rejected by the compiler, TS2345):
 type EdgeB = [to: number, w: number];
function addBad(adj: EdgeB[][], u: number, v: number): void {
  (adj[u] as EdgeB[]).push([v]);
}
Correct:
 type Edge = [to: number, w: number];
class WGraph {
  adj: Edge[][];
  constructor(readonly n: number) {
    this.adj = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [] as Edge[]);
  }
  addEdge(u: number, v: number, w: number): void {
    (this.adj[u] as Edge[]).push([v, w]);
  }
}
An undirected weighted edge is stored in both directions with the same weight
ts-weightedgraph-undirected   lens: mutation   level: L1   family: ts-wg-mutation
Prerequisites: ts-weightedgraph-edge-type. Follow-up: ts-weightedgraph-dijkstra. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. addEdge(u, v, w) is undirected by default.
The basic addEdge stores one directed arc. For an undirected weighted edge add the arc in both directions with the
same weight. Adding only one arc makes algorithms such as Prim's tree and shortest paths from the other end miss
the edge.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function addUndirectedBad(g: WGraph, u: number, v: number, w: number): void {
  g.addEdge(u, v, w);
}
Correct:
 function addUndirected(g: WGraph, u: number, v: number, w: number): void {
  g.addEdge(u, v, w);
  g.addEdge(v, u, w);
}

