4.4 Algorithm chunks
Dijkstra finalizes the unvisited vertex with the smallest distance, not the first one
ts-weightedgraph-dijkstra   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-wg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-weightedgraph-undirected. Follow-up: ts-weightedgraph-bellman-ford. Complexity: O(V^2).
 Common misconception. Process vertices in index order and relax their edges.
The correctness of Dijkstra rests on one rule: the unfinished vertex with the smallest tentative distance already has its
final distance, as long as weights are not negative. Picking any other vertex, such as the lowest index, finalizes
distances that a cheaper path would still improve.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function dijkstraBad(g: WGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  const done = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (let step = 0; step < g.n; step++) {
    const u = done.indexOf(false);
    done[u] = true;
    for (const [v, w] of g.adj[u] as Edge[]) {
      const nd = (dist[u] as number) + w;
      if (!done[v] && nd < (dist[v] as number)) dist[v] = nd;
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
Correct:
 function dijkstra(g: WGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  const done = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (let step = 0; step < g.n; step++) {
    let u = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < g.n; i++) {
      if (!done[i] && (u === -1 || (dist[i] as number) < (dist[u] as number))) u = i;
    }
    if (u === -1 || dist[u] === Infinity) break;
    done[u] = true;
    for (const [v, w] of g.adj[u] as Edge[]) {
      if ((dist[u] as number) + w < (dist[v] as number)) dist[v] = (dist[u] as number) + w;
    }
  }
  return dist;
}

TypeScript Weighted Graph: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 6
Prim keeps the cheapest single edge into the tree; adding path lengths turns it into Dijkstra
ts-weightedgraph-prim   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-wg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-weightedgraph-bellman-ford. Follow-up: ts-weightedgraph-floyd. Complexity: O(V^2).
 Common misconception. The key of a vertex is its distance from the start, as in Dijkstra.
Prim grows a tree by repeatedly adding the vertex with the cheapest single edge to the tree, so the key is the edge
weight alone. Using key[u] + w, the distance from the root, builds the shortest path tree instead and returns a larger
total.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function primBad(g: WGraph): number {
  const key = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  const inTree = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  key[0] = 0;
  let total = 0;
  for (let step = 0; step < g.n; step++) {
    let u = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < g.n; i++) {
      if (!inTree[i] && (u === -1 || (key[i] as number) < (key[u] as number))) u = i;
    }
    if (u === -1 || key[u] === Infinity) break;
    inTree[u] = true;
    total += key[u] as number;
    for (const [v, w] of g.adj[u] as Edge[]) {
      const nk = (key[u] as number) + w;
      if (!inTree[v] && nk < (key[v] as number)) key[v] = nk;
    }
  }
  return total;
}
Correct:
 function prim(g: WGraph): number {
  const key = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  const inTree = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  key[0] = 0;
  let total = 0;
  for (let step = 0; step < g.n; step++) {
    let u = -1;
    for (let i = 0; i < g.n; i++) {
      if (!inTree[i] && (u === -1 || (key[i] as number) < (key[u] as number))) u = i;
    }
    if (u === -1 || key[u] === Infinity) break;
    inTree[u] = true;
    total += key[u] as number;
    for (const [v, w] of g.adj[u] as Edge[]) {
      if (!inTree[v] && w < (key[v] as number)) key[v] = w;
    }
  }
  return total;
}

TypeScript Weighted Graph: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 7
Bellman-Ford needs one extra round to detect a negative cycle
ts-weightedgraph-bellman-ford   lens: patterns   level: L4   family: ts-wg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-weightedgraph-dijkstra. Follow-up: ts-weightedgraph-prim. Complexity: O(V E).
 Common misconception. V - 1 rounds of relaxation are enough, and the result is always valid.
With no negative cycle, V - 1 rounds give the final distances. If a V-th round still improves some distance, a negative
cycle is reachable and distances are meaningless. A version that stops after V - 1 rounds and returns the array hides
that case.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function bellmanFordBad(g: WGraph, src: number): number[] | null {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (let round = 0; round < g.n - 1; round++) {
    g.adj.forEach((row, u) => {
      for (const [v, w] of row) {
        if ((dist[u] as number) + w < (dist[v] as number)) dist[v] = (dist[u] as number) + w;
      }
    });
  }
  return dist;
}
Correct:
 function bellmanFord(g: WGraph, src: number): number[] | null {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(Infinity);
  dist[src] = 0;
  for (let round = 0; round < g.n; round++) {
    let changed = false;
    g.adj.forEach((row, u) => {
      for (const [v, w] of row) {
        if ((dist[u] as number) + w < (dist[v] as number)) {
          dist[v] = (dist[u] as number) + w;
          changed = true;
        }
      }
    });
    if (!changed) return dist;
  }
  return null;
}

TypeScript Weighted Graph: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 8
Floyd-Warshall must loop over the intermediate vertex k on the outside
ts-weightedgraph-floyd   lens: patterns   level: L4   family: ts-wg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-weightedgraph-prim. Follow-up: none. Complexity: O(V^3).
 Common misconception. The three loops can be nested in any order.
The recurrence considers paths that use only the first k vertices as intermediates, so k must be the outermost loop.
With k innermost, a cell is combined with cells that are not final yet, and paths that need several intermediates are
missed.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function floydBad(g: WGraph): number[][] {
  const d = Array.from({ length: g.n }, (_, i) =>
    Array.from({ length: g.n }, (_, j) => (i === j ? 0 : Infinity)));
  g.adj.forEach((row, u) => row.forEach(([v, w]) => {
    if (w < ((d[u] as number[])[v] as number)) (d[u] as number[])[v] = w;
  }));
  for (let i = 0; i < g.n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < g.n; j++) {
      for (let k = 0; k < g.n; k++) {
        const via = ((d[i] as number[])[k] as number) + ((d[k] as number[])[j] as number);
        if (via < ((d[i] as number[])[j] as number)) (d[i] as number[])[j] = via;
      }
    }
  }
  return d;
}
Correct:
 function floyd(g: WGraph): number[][] {
  const d = Array.from({ length: g.n }, (_, i) =>
    Array.from({ length: g.n }, (_, j) => (i === j ? 0 : Infinity)));
  g.adj.forEach((row, u) => row.forEach(([v, w]) => {
    if (w < ((d[u] as number[])[v] as number)) (d[u] as number[])[v] = w;
  }));
  for (let k = 0; k < g.n; k++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < g.n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < g.n; j++) {
        const via = ((d[i] as number[])[k] as number) + ((d[k] as number[])[j] as number);
        if (via < ((d[i] as number[])[j] as number)) (d[i] as number[])[j] = via;
      }
    }
  }
  return d;
}

