Prompt. A pop implementation always swaps the root with its left child while the left child is smaller. Explain why the
heap property can break.
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). The root must become no larger than both children. If the right child is
even smaller than the left one, swapping with the left leaves a larger value above a smaller sibling, so the parent-child
invariant is false. Compare both children, swap with the smaller (for a min-heap) and stop when neither is smaller.
