The bottom-up query takes a half-open range, so an inclusive end needs + 1
ts-segmenttree-half-open   lens: access   level: L3   family: ts-seg-access
Prerequisites: ts-segmenttree-min-identity. Follow-up: ts-segmenttree-lazy. Complexity: O(log n).
 Common misconception. query(l, r) includes the element at r.
The iterative bottom-up loop runs while l < r, so the range is [l, r) and element r is excluded. A caller who thinks in
inclusive ranges loses the last element. Convert at the boundary with r + 1, in one wrapper, so the rest of the code
uses one convention.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function minInclusiveBad(s: MinSeg, l: number, r: number): number {
  return s.query(l, r);
}
Correct:
 function minInclusive(s: MinSeg, l: number, r: number): number {
  return s.query(l, r + 1);
}
The starting value of a min query is Infinity; 0 wins against every positive number
ts-segmenttree-min-identity   lens: edge-cases   level: L3   family: ts-seg-edge-cases
Prerequisites: ts-segmenttree-update. Follow-up: ts-segmenttree-half-open. Complexity: O(log n).
 Common misconception. A running minimum can start at 0.
The identity of min is Infinity, because min(Infinity, x) is x. Starting from 0 returns 0 for any list of positive numbers.
The same holds for padding unused slots of the tree. Use 0 for sum, 1 for product, Infinity for min and -Infinity for
max.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function minBad(t: number[], n: number, l: number, r: number): number {
  let res = 0;
  for (l += n, r += n; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
    if (l & 1) res = Math.min(res, t[l++] as number);
    if (r & 1) res = Math.min(res, t[--r] as number);
  }
  return res;
}

TypeScript Segment Tree: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 7
Correct:
 class MinSeg {
  n: number;
  t: number[];
  constructor(a: number[]) {
    this.n = a.length;
    this.t = new Array<number>(2 * this.n).fill(Infinity);
    a.forEach((v, i) => { this.t[this.n + i] = v; });
    for (let i = this.n - 1; i >= 1; i--) {
      this.t[i] = Math.min(this.t[2 * i] as number, this.t[2 * i + 1] as number);
    }
  }
  query(l: number, r: number): number {
    let res = Infinity;
    for (l += this.n, r += this.n; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.min(res, this.t[l++] as number);
      if (r & 1) res = Math.min(res, this.t[--r] as number);
    }
    return res;
  }
}
Lazy propagation pushes pending range adds to the children before a query descends
ts-segmenttree-lazy   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-seg-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-segmenttree-half-open. Follow-up: none. Complexity: O(log n).
 Common misconception. A query can read child sums directly after a range add.
A range add that covers a node completely updates only that node and stores a pending tag for its children. A later
query that descends below this node must push the tag down first, or the children still hold the old sums. Skipping the
push gives stale results.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function queryNoPush(
  t: LazySeg, ql: number, qr: number, node = 1, l = 0, r = t.n - 1,
): number {
  if (qr < l || r < ql) return 0;
  if (ql <= l && r <= qr) return t.sum[node] as number;
  const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
  const left = queryNoPush(t, ql, qr, 2 * node, l, mid);
  return left + queryNoPush(t, ql, qr, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
}

TypeScript Segment Tree: Training Data for the DSA Mentor RAG Chatbot
Page 8
Correct:
 class LazySeg {
  n: number;
  sum: number[];
  lazy: number[];
  constructor(n: number) {
    this.n = n;
    this.sum = new Array<number>(4 * n).fill(0);
    this.lazy = new Array<number>(4 * n).fill(0);
  }
  apply(node: number, l: number, r: number, d: number): void {
    this.sum[node] = (this.sum[node] as number) + d * (r - l + 1);
    this.lazy[node] = (this.lazy[node] as number) + d;
  }
  push(node: number, l: number, r: number): void {
    const d = this.lazy[node] as number;
    if (d === 0) return;
    const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
    this.apply(2 * node, l, mid, d);
    this.apply(2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r, d);
    this.lazy[node] = 0;
  }
  rangeAdd(ql: number, qr: number, d: number, node = 1, l = 0, r = this.n - 1): void {
    if (qr < l || r < ql) return;
    if (ql <= l && r <= qr) { this.apply(node, l, r, d); return; }
    this.push(node, l, r);
    const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
    this.rangeAdd(ql, qr, d, 2 * node, l, mid);
    this.rangeAdd(ql, qr, d, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
    this.sum[node] = (this.sum[2 * node] as number) + (this.sum[2 * node + 1] as number);
  }
  query(ql: number, qr: number, node = 1, l = 0, r = this.n - 1): number {
    if (qr < l || r < ql) return 0;
    if (ql <= l && r <= qr) return this.sum[node] as number;
    this.push(node, l, r);
    const mid = (l + r) >> 1;
    const left = this.query(ql, qr, 2 * node, l, mid);
    return left + this.query(ql, qr, 2 * node + 1, mid + 1, r);
  }
}
