2. Overview and mental model: Deque
A deque supports push and pop at both ends in O(1). JavaScript arrays are O(1) at the back only; unshift and shift
move every element. A deque keeps a head and a tail index (here in a Map, or in a circular buffer) so both ends are
cheap.
The typing rule is the same as for stacks and queues: pops and peeks return T | undefined, which the compiler forces
you to handle.
Deques solve sliding window problems (monotonic deque), 0-1 BFS, palindromes and work stealing.
Toolbox
