peekFront and peekBack return T | undefined, so using the result directly is error TS2532
ts-deque-peek   lens: access   level: L1   family: ts-deque-access
Prerequisites: ts-deque-empty. Follow-up: ts-deque-rotate. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. The front element of a deque of numbers can be used without a check.
An empty deque has no front, so the return type includes undefined. Calling a method on it is error TS2532. Test size
first, or use optional chaining (d.peekFront()?.toFixed(1)).
Incorrect (rejected by the compiler, TS2532):
 function fixedBad(d: Deque<number>): string {
  return d.peekFront().toFixed(1);
}
Correct:
 function fixed(d: Deque<number>): string | undefined {
  return d.peekFront()?.toFixed(1);
}
Popping an empty deque must not move the indexes, or size goes negative
ts-deque-empty   lens: edge-cases   level: L1   family: ts-deque-edge
Prerequisites: ts-deque-core. Follow-up: ts-deque-peek. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. popBack can decrement tail without checking whether the deque is empty.
On an empty deque head equals tail. Decrementing tail below head makes size negative, and later pushes land in the
wrong slots. Guard every pop with an emptiness test and return undefined.
