4.4 Algorithm chunks
Rotate by k mod size, normalised for negative k
ts-deque-rotate   lens: mutation   level: L2   family: ts-deque-mutation
Prerequisites: ts-deque-peek. Follow-up: ts-deque-sliding-max. Complexity: O(n).
 Common misconception. Looping k times rotates correctly for any integer k.
A negative k never runs the loop, so nothing rotates, and a huge k repeats full turns. Reduce first: steps = ((k % n) +
n) % n, then move steps items from the back to the front.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 function rotateBad<T>(d: Deque<T>, k: number): void {
  for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) d.pushFront(d.popBack() as T);
}
Correct:
 function rotate<T>(d: Deque<T>, k: number): void {
  const n = d.size;
  if (n === 0) return;
  const steps = ((k % n) + n) % n;
  for (let i = 0; i < steps; i++) d.pushFront(d.popBack() as T);
}

