ts-deque-sliding-max-ex2   format: choose-the-structure   level: L3   grading: rubric   modes: practice, interview
Prompt. Explain why a deque gives O(n) sliding window maximum and why indexes (not values) are stored.
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). The deque keeps indexes of candidate maxima in decreasing value
order: the front is the current maximum. When a new value arrives, smaller values at the back can never be a maximum
again and are popped. Storing indexes lets you drop the front when it falls out of the window (index <= i - k). Each index
is pushed and popped at most once, so the total is O(n); recomputing the maximum for every window is O(nk).
