2. Overview and mental model: Doubly Linked List
A doubly linked list keeps prev and next pointers on every node, plus head and tail pointers on the list. That makes
appending, prepending and removing a known node O(1), at the cost of an extra pointer per node.
All the difficulty is in the four or six pointer updates and in the edge cases where a pointer is null: the head, the tail
and the single node. The compiler helps with null checks (DNode | null), but not with forgetting to update a link.
Pair it with a Map from key to node to build LRU caches and similar structures with O(1) operations.
Toolbox
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
const list = new DoublyLinkedList<number>();
const node = list.pushBack(1);      // returns the node, so callers can remove it later
list.pushFront(0);
removeNode(list, node);              // O(1)
