Reversing swaps prev and next on every node and also swaps head and tail
ts-doublylinkedlist-reverse   lens: mutation   level: L2   family: ts-dll-reversal
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-insert-after. Follow-up: ts-doublylinkedlist-lru. Complexity: O(n).
 Common misconception. Swapping prev and next on each node is enough to reverse the list.
After the swaps the old tail is the first node, so head and tail must also be exchanged. Forgetting that leaves a list
whose head points at the old first node, whose next is now null. Walk with the saved next pointer, because swapping
changes which field goes forward.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function reverseBad<T>(l: DLike<T>): void {
  for (let n = l.head; n; n = n.prev) [n.prev, n.next] = [n.next, n.prev];
}
Correct:
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function reverse<T>(l: DLike<T>): void {
  let n = l.head;
  while (n) {
    const next = n.next;
    [n.prev, n.next] = [n.next, n.prev];
    n = next;
  }
  [l.head, l.tail] = [l.tail, l.head];
}
Inserting after a node needs four pointer updates; skipping next.prev breaks backward
traversal
ts-doublylinkedlist-insert-after   lens: insertion-and-deletion   level: L2   family: ts-dll-insert
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-remove-node. Follow-up: ts-doublylinkedlist-reverse. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. Linking the new node forward is enough for a correct list.
Inserting after node n changes four links: new.prev = n, new.next = n.next, n.next.prev = new and n.next = new.
Missing n.next.prev leaves the following node pointing back at n, so reversed traversal skips the new value. If n was
the tail, the tail pointer must move.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function insertAfterBad<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>, value: T): DNode<T> {
  const node: DNode<T> = { value, prev: n, next: n.next };
  n.next = node;
  if (!node.next) l.tail = node;
  l.size++;
  return node;
}

