Correct:
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function insertAfter<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>, value: T): DNode<T> {
  const node: DNode<T> = { value, prev: n, next: n.next };
  if (n.next) n.next.prev = node;
  else l.tail = node;
  n.next = node;
  l.size++;
  return node;
}
Removing a node in O(1) must handle the head and tail, where prev or next is null
ts-doublylinkedlist-remove-node   lens: insertion-and-deletion   level: L2   family: ts-dll-delete
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-pop-edge. Follow-up: ts-doublylinkedlist-insert-after. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. node.prev.next = node.next works for every node.
The first node has no prev and the last has no next, so those assignments throw TypeError. Branch on each side:
update the neighbour if there is one, otherwise move the list head or tail. Also clear the removed node links so it
cannot be reused by accident.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNodeBad<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
  n.prev!.next = n.next;
  n.next!.prev = n.prev;
  l.size--;
}
Correct:
 type DNode<T> = { value: T; prev: DNode<T> | null; next: DNode<T> | null };
type DLike<T> = { head: DNode<T> | null; tail: DNode<T> | null; size: number };
function removeNode<T>(l: DLike<T>, n: DNode<T>): void {
  if (n.prev) n.prev.next = n.next;
  else l.head = n.next;
  if (n.next) n.next.prev = n.prev;
  else l.tail = n.prev;
  n.prev = n.next = null;
  l.size--;
}

