An LRU cache pairs a hash map with a doubly linked list so reads and evictions are O(1)
ts-doublylinkedlist-lru   lens: patterns   level: L3   family: ts-dll-patterns
Prerequisites: ts-doublylinkedlist-reverse. Follow-up: none. Complexity: O(1).
 Common misconception. A Map alone gives O(1) recency updates for any key type.
A Map can track recency through insertion order, but a hand-built LRU with a doubly linked list and a Map from key to
node is the classic design for interviews and for languages without ordered maps. On get, unlink the node and push it
to the front; on put, insert at the front and evict the tail when full. A get that does not move the node makes the cache
FIFO.
Incorrect (runs, but fails as described):
 class LruBad<K, V> {
  private map = new Map<K, V>();
  private order: K[] = [];
  constructor(private cap: number) {}
  get(k: K): V | undefined {
    return this.map.get(k);
  }
  put(k: K, v: V): void {
    if (!this.map.has(k)) this.order.push(k);
    this.map.set(k, v);
    if (this.order.length > this.cap) this.map.delete(this.order.shift() as K);
  }
}
Correct:
 class LruCache<K, V> {
  private list = new DoublyLinkedList<[K, V]>();
  private map = new Map<K, DNode<[K, V]>>();
  constructor(private cap: number) {}
  get(k: K): V | undefined {
    const node = this.map.get(k);
    if (!node) return undefined;
    removeNode(this.list, node);
    this.map.set(k, this.list.pushFront(node.value));
    return node.value[1];
  }
  put(k: K, v: V): void {
    const old = this.map.get(k);
    if (old) removeNode(this.list, old);
    this.map.set(k, this.list.pushFront([k, v]));
    if (this.list.size > this.cap) {
      const last = this.list.tail as DNode<[K, V]>;
      removeNode(this.list, last);
      this.map.delete(last.value[0]);
    }
  }
}

