ts-doublylinkedlist-pop-edge-ex1   format: spot-the-bug   level: L1   grading: judge   modes: practice, interview
Prompt. popBackBad throws on a one-element list. Explain why and give the fix.
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). After l.tail = last.prev, the tail is null for a single node, so l.tail!.next
throws TypeError. The fix is to check: if there is a new tail, set its next to null, otherwise the list is empty and head must
be set to null as well.
ts-doublylinkedlist-core-ex2   format: choose-the-structure   level: L2   grading: rubric   modes: practice, interview
Prompt. Compare a doubly linked list with a singly linked list and an array for removing a known element from the
middle.
Answer key (mentor only, never embedded). With a reference to the node, a doubly linked list removes it in O(1)
because prev and next are available; a singly linked list needs the predecessor, found in O(n); an array shifts later
elements, O(n). The doubly linked list pays an extra pointer per node and loses cache locality, so use it for LRU caches,
editors and queues with arbitrary removal.
