TypeScript · array · transformation · L2
Concept: filter(Boolean) does not narrow the element type; use a type predicate, which TypeScript 5.5 can infer
Misconception: arr.filter(Boolean) turns (number | undefined)[] into number[].
Explanation: The compiler does not know that Boolean removes undefined, so the result is still (number | undefined)[]. Boolean also removes 0 and empty strings, which is usually a bug. Write filter((x): x is number => x !== undefined). Since TypeScript 5.5 the arrow function x => x !== undefined is inferred as a type predicate and narrows by itself.
Incorrect (compile error TS2322):
function compactBad(xs: (number | undefined)[]): number[] {
  return xs.filter(Boolean);
}
Correct:
function compact(xs: readonly (number | undefined)[]): number[] {
  return xs.filter((x): x is number => x !== undefined);
}
Complexity: O(n)

