TypeScript · array · equality · L2
Concept: includes on a readonly tuple of literals rejects a plain string; write a type guard that widens the array once
Misconception: ALLOWED.includes(x) is the way to check that a string is one of the allowed literals.
Explanation: With const ALLOWED = ["a", "b"] as const, includes expects "a" | "b", so passing a string is error TS2345. Widen the array to readonly string[] inside a small type guard, function isAllowed(x: string): x is Allowed. The guard then narrows x everywhere it is used, which a plain boolean cannot.
Incorrect (compile error TS2345):
const ALLOWED = ["a", "b"] as const;
function isAllowedBad(x: string): boolean {
  return ALLOWED.includes(x);
}
Correct:
const ALLOWED = ["a", "b"] as const;
type Allowed = (typeof ALLOWED)[number];
function isAllowed(x: string): x is Allowed {
  return (ALLOWED as readonly string[]).includes(x);
}
Complexity: O(n)

