TypeScript · array · access · L0
Concept: Indexing an array is typed T even when the index is out of range; at() and a check give T | undefined
Misconception: nums[i] is always a number, because the array is typed number[].
Explanation: By default TypeScript types nums[i] as number, but an index past the end gives undefined at runtime, and arithmetic on it yields NaN. The compiler only reports it if you turn on noUncheckedIndexedAccess. Array.prototype.at(i) is typed number | undefined, and also accepts negative indexes, so the compiler makes you handle the missing case.
Incorrect:
function lastBad(a: number[]): number {
  return a[a.length];
}
Correct:
function last(a: readonly number[]): number | undefined {
  return a.at(-1);
}
function lastOr(a: readonly number[], fallback: number): number {
  return a.at(-1) ?? fallback;
}
Complexity: O(1)

