Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · array · transformation · L2
Concept: reduce without an initial value throws on an empty array; always pass one, and annotate its type
Misconception: xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b) is a safe way to sum.
Explanation: Without an initial value reduce uses the first element as the accumulator and throws TypeError on an empty array. The type checker does not warn, because the callback types are fine. Pass an initial value, and give it a type when the result differs from the element type, for example reduce<Record<string, number>>((acc, x) => ..., {}).
Incorrect:
function sumBad(xs: number[]): number {
  return xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b);
}
Correct:
function sum(xs: readonly number[]): number {
  return xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
}
function countBy(xs: readonly string[]): Record<string, number> {
  return xs.reduce<Record<string, number>>((acc, x) => {
    acc[x] = (acc[x] ?? 0) + 1;
    return acc;
  }, {});
}
Complexity: O(n)

