TypeScript · array · mutation · L1
Concept: Spread and slice make a shallow copy: the outer array is new but nested arrays are shared
Misconception: const copy = [...grid] gives an independent copy of a two-dimensional array.
Explanation: Spread, slice and Array.from copy only the first level. The inner arrays are the same objects in both, so writing copy[0][0] changes the original too. Copy each level you intend to change, for example grid.map(row => [...row]), or use structuredClone for plain data. The type readonly number[][] does not protect the inner arrays; use readonly (readonly number[])[].
Incorrect:
function setBad(grid: number[][]): number[][] {
  const copy = [...grid];
  copy[0]![0] = 99;
  return copy;
}
Correct:
function setCell(grid: readonly (readonly number[])[], v: number): number[][] {
  const copy = grid.map(row => [...row]);
  copy[0]![0] = v;
  return copy;
}
Complexity: O(rows * cols)

