TypeScript · arraybuffer · insertion-and-deletion · L2
Concept: Only a buffer created with maxByteLength can resize; a fixed buffer throws TypeError
Misconception: Any ArrayBuffer can grow with buf.resize(n).
Explanation: A normal ArrayBuffer is fixed in size. resize works only on a resizable buffer, created with new ArrayBuffer(n, { maxByteLength }), and never beyond that maximum. A typed array created with no explicit length on a resizable buffer tracks the length automatically. Otherwise, allocate a bigger buffer and copy.
Incorrect:
function growBad(buf: ArrayBuffer): void {
  buf.resize(buf.byteLength * 2);
}
Correct:
function makeGrowable(n: number, max: number): ArrayBuffer {
  return new ArrayBuffer(n, { maxByteLength: max });
}
function growTo(buf: ArrayBuffer, n: number): boolean {
  if (!buf.resizable || n > buf.maxByteLength) return false;
  buf.resize(n);
  return true;
}
Complexity: O(n)

