TypeScript · binarytree · patterns · L3
Concept: The lowest common ancestor is the node whose two subtrees each contain one target
Misconception: Return whichever side found something.
Explanation: If both the left and the right search return a node, the current node is the split point and the answer. Returning left ?? right at every level passes a target upward and skips the split, which returns one of the targets instead of their ancestor.
Incorrect:
function lcaBad<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null, p: T, q: T): TreeNode<T> | null {
  if (root === null || root.val === p || root.val === q) return root;
  const l = lcaBad(root.left, p, q);
  const r = lcaBad(root.right, p, q);
  return l ?? r;
}
Correct:
function lca<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null, p: T, q: T): TreeNode<T> | null {
  if (root === null || root.val === p || root.val === q) return root;
  const l = lca(root.left, p, q);
  const r = lca(root.right, p, q);
  if (l !== null && r !== null) return root;
  return l ?? r;
}
Complexity: O(n)

