Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · binarytree · iteration · L2
Concept: Level order processes one level at a time; one flat queue loop mixes the levels
Misconception: A single loop over a growing queue gives the levels.
Explanation: A queue loop visits nodes in level order but does not say where a level ends, so everything lands in one row. Keep the current level in an array, read its values, then build the next level from its children, and repeat until a level is empty.
Incorrect:
function levelsBad<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null): T[][] {
  const out: T[][] = [];
  if (root === null) return out;
  const queue: Array<TreeNode<T>> = [root];
  const row: T[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    const n = queue[i] as TreeNode<T>;
    row.push(n.val);
    if (n.left) queue.push(n.left);
    if (n.right) queue.push(n.right);
  }
  out.push(row);
  return out;
}
Correct:
function levels<T>(root: TreeNode<T> | null): T[][] {
  const out: T[][] = [];
  let level: Array<TreeNode<T>> = root ? [root] : [];
  while (level.length > 0) {
    out.push(level.map((n) => n.val));
    level = level.flatMap((n) => [n.left, n.right])
      .filter((c): c is TreeNode<T> => c !== null);
  }
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n)

