TypeScript · binarytree · access · L1
Concept: Child links are TreeNode | null, so strict mode rejects access that skips the null check
Misconception: n.left.val is safe once the tree is built.
Explanation: A leaf has no children, so left and right are typed TreeNode<T> | null. With strictNullChecks the compiler rejects n.left.val (TS18047) because the link may be null. Use optional chaining with a fallback, or test the link before reading it.
Incorrect (compile error TS18047):
interface NodeB {
  val: number;
  left: NodeB | null;
}
function leftBad(n: NodeB): number {
  return n.left.val;
}
Correct:
interface TreeNode<T> {
  val: T;
  left: TreeNode<T> | null;
  right: TreeNode<T> | null;
}
function node<T>(
  val: T, left: TreeNode<T> | null = null, right: TreeNode<T> | null = null,
): TreeNode<T> {
  return { val, left, right };
}
function leftVal(n: TreeNode<number>): number {
  return n.left?.val ?? -1;
}
Complexity: O(1)

