Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · btree · iteration · L2
Concept: An internal node with k keys has k+1 children, so in-order traversal visits one more child after the last key
Misconception: Visit child i, then key i, for every key.
Explanation: A node with k keys has k+1 children. A loop over the keys visits the first k children; the last child is to the right of the last key and needs a separate visit after the loop. Forgetting it drops the largest keys of every internal node.
Incorrect:
function inorderBad(x: BNode): number[] {
  const out: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < x.keys.length; i++) {
    if (!x.leaf) out.push(...inorderBad(x.children[i] as BNode));
    out.push(x.keys[i] as number);
  }
  return out;
}
Correct:
function inorder(x: BNode): number[] {
  const out: number[] = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < x.keys.length; i++) {
    if (!x.leaf) out.push(...inorder(x.children[i] as BNode));
    out.push(x.keys[i] as number);
  }
  if (!x.leaf) out.push(...inorder(x.children[x.keys.length] as BNode));
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n)

