Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · btree · access · L1
Concept: Search checks for an equal key in every node, because keys live in internal nodes too
Misconception: Search can always descend to a leaf.
Explanation: Unlike a B+ tree, a B-tree stores keys in internal nodes. After finding the first key that is not smaller than the target, check whether it is equal and stop; otherwise the search descends into the child left of that key and misses the key.
Incorrect:
function searchBad(x: BNode, k: number): boolean {
  let i = 0;
  while (i < x.keys.length && k > (x.keys[i] as number)) i++;
  if (x.leaf) return i < x.keys.length && x.keys[i] === k;
  return searchBad(x.children[i] as BNode, k);
}
Correct:
class BNode {
  keys: number[] = [];
  children: BNode[] = [];
  constructor(public leaf: boolean) {}
}
function search(x: BNode, k: number): boolean {
  let i = 0;
  while (i < x.keys.length && k > (x.keys[i] as number)) i++;
  if (i < x.keys.length && x.keys[i] === k) return true;
  if (x.leaf) return false;
  return search(x.children[i] as BNode, k);
}
Complexity: O(t log_t n)

