TypeScript · dataview · access · L1
Concept: DataView reads big-endian by default; typed arrays use the platform order, usually little-endian
Misconception: Reading two bytes through a Uint16Array and through a DataView give the same number.
Explanation: Network protocols and file formats usually use big-endian. DataView methods default to big-endian and take a littleEndian flag. A Uint16Array uses the CPU order, which is little-endian on x86 and ARM. The bytes [1, 2] are 258 big-endian and 513 little-endian.
Incorrect:
function readU16Bad(buf: ArrayBuffer): number {
  return new Uint16Array(buf)[0] ?? 0;
}
Correct:
function readU16(buf: ArrayBuffer): number {
  return new DataView(buf).getUint16(0);
}
Complexity: O(1)

