TypeScript · dataview · equality · L2
Concept: setFloat32 rounds to single precision, so comparing the read value with the original often fails; compare with Math.fround
Misconception: Writing 0.1 with setFloat32 and reading it back gives 0.1.
Explanation: Float32 has about seven decimal digits. 0.1 reads back as 0.10000000149011612. Compare to Math.fround(x), which rounds a number to float32, or compare with a tolerance. Use setFloat64 when you need double precision.
Incorrect:
function roundTripBad(x: number): boolean {
  const dv = new DataView(new ArrayBuffer(4));
  dv.setFloat32(0, x);
  return dv.getFloat32(0) === x;
}
Correct:
function roundTrip(x: number): boolean {
  const dv = new DataView(new ArrayBuffer(4));
  dv.setFloat32(0, x);
  return dv.getFloat32(0) === Math.fround(x);
}
Complexity: O(1)

