TypeScript · deque · patterns · L3
Concept: Sliding window maximum keeps a decreasing deque of indexes and drops the front when it leaves the window
Misconception: Keeping a decreasing deque is enough; the front is always inside the window.
Explanation: The deque holds indexes whose values decrease from front to back, so the front is the maximum. The front can be an old index that has slid out of the window, and then it must be removed. Forgetting that check returns a stale maximum. Each index is pushed and popped once: O(n).
Incorrect:
function maxSlidingBad(nums: number[], k: number): number[] {
  const dq = new Deque<number>();
  const out: number[] = [];
  nums.forEach((x, i) => {
    while (dq.size && (nums[dq.peekBack() as number] as number) <= x) dq.popBack();
    dq.pushBack(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) out.push(nums[dq.peekFront() as number] as number);
  });
  return out;
}
Correct:
function maxSliding(nums: number[], k: number): number[] {
  const dq = new Deque<number>();
  const out: number[] = [];
  nums.forEach((x, i) => {
    if (dq.size && (dq.peekFront() as number) <= i - k) dq.popFront();
    while (dq.size && (nums[dq.peekBack() as number] as number) <= x) dq.popBack();
    dq.pushBack(i);
    if (i >= k - 1) out.push(nums[dq.peekFront() as number] as number);
  });
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(n)

