TypeScript · directedgraph · patterns · L2
Concept: Shortest hop count needs BFS; the first path found by DFS can be much longer
Misconception: Any traversal that sets dist when it first reaches a node gives the shortest distance.
Explanation: BFS reaches nodes in order of distance, so the first time a node is reached is the shortest. DFS follows one branch to the end, so the first time it reaches a node may be through a long path. Use a queue for unweighted shortest paths.
Incorrect:
function dfsDistBad(g: DiGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(-1);
  const go = (u: number, d: number): void => {
    dist[u] = d;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) if (dist[v] === -1) go(v, d + 1);
  };
  go(src, 0);
  return dist;
}
Correct:
function bfsDist(g: DiGraph, src: number): number[] {
  const dist = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(-1);
  dist[src] = 0;
  const queue = [src];
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    const u = queue[i] as number;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) {
      if (dist[v] === -1) {
        dist[v] = (dist[u] as number) + 1;
        queue.push(v);
      }
    }
  }
  return dist;
}
Complexity: O(V + E)

