TypeScript · directedgraph · ordering · L3
Concept: A DFS finishing order must be reversed to be a topological order; Kahn removes sources
Misconception: The order in which DFS finishes nodes is a topological order.
Explanation: DFS finishes a node after all of its descendants, so the finishing order lists dependencies first, which is the reverse of what a schedule needs. Reverse it, or use Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly take a vertex with indegree zero. If fewer than n vertices come out, the graph has a cycle.
Incorrect:
function topoBad(g: DiGraph): number[] {
  const seen = new Array<boolean>(g.n).fill(false);
  const out: number[] = [];
  const visit = (u: number): void => {
    seen[u] = true;
    for (const v of g.adj[u] as number[]) if (!seen[v]) visit(v);
    out.push(u);
  };
  for (let u = 0; u < g.n; u++) if (!seen[u]) visit(u);
  return out;
}
Correct:
function topoSort(g: DiGraph): number[] {
  const indeg = new Array<number>(g.n).fill(0);
  for (const row of g.adj) for (const v of row) indeg[v] = (indeg[v] as number) + 1;
  const queue: number[] = [];
  indeg.forEach((d, u) => { if (d === 0) queue.push(u); });
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {
    for (const v of g.adj[queue[i] as number] as number[]) {
      indeg[v] = (indeg[v] as number) - 1;
      if (indeg[v] === 0) queue.push(v);
    }
  }
  return queue.length === g.n ? queue : [];
}
Complexity: O(V + E)

