TypeScript · disjointset · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Detect a cycle by testing whether union merged two sets; testing connectivity after the union is always true
Misconception: After union(a, b), connected(a, b) tells whether the edge closed a cycle.
Explanation: Immediately after union the two endpoints are connected, so the check always reports a cycle. Detect the cycle before merging, or use the boolean returned by union: false means the endpoints were already connected, so the edge creates a cycle.
Incorrect:
function hasCycleBad(n: number, edges: [number, number][]): boolean {
  const uf = new UnionFind(n);
  for (const [a, b] of edges) {
    uf.union(a, b);
    if (uf.connected(a, b)) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
Correct:
function hasCycle(n: number, edges: [number, number][]): boolean {
  const uf = new UnionFind(n);
  for (const [a, b] of edges) if (!uf.union(a, b)) return true;
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(E alpha(n))

