TypeScript · enum · fit-and-alternatives · L2
Concept: A const enum leaves no object at run time, so it cannot be iterated or passed around as a value
Misconception: A const enum is a normal enum with a speed benefit, so Object.keys(Mode) works.
Explanation: const enum members are replaced by their values at compile time and the enum object is not emitted. Using the enum as a value (Object.keys(Mode), passing it as an argument) is error TS2475. Use a regular enum, or an as const object, when the set of members must exist at run time.
Incorrect (compile error TS2475):
const enum Mode { Read, Write }
const keysBad = Object.keys(Mode);
Correct:
const MODE = { Read: 0, Write: 1 } as const;
type Mode = (typeof MODE)[keyof typeof MODE];
function modeNames(): string[] {
  return Object.keys(MODE);
}
Complexity: O(1)

