TypeScript · enum · testing · L3
Concept: Add an assertNever default so a new enum member turns a forgotten case into a compile error
Misconception: A switch over an enum with no default is checked for missing members.
Explanation: The compiler accepts a switch that handles only some members and returns undefined for the rest. Adding a default branch that passes the value to a function taking never turns a missing case into error TS2345 as soon as the enum gains a member.
Incorrect (compile error TS2345):
enum ShapeB { Circle, Square }
function assertNeverB(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(String(x));
}
function sidesBad(s: ShapeB): number {
  switch (s) {
    case ShapeB.Circle: return 0;
    default: return assertNeverB(s);
  }
}
Correct:
enum Shape { Circle, Square }
function assertNever(x: never): never {
  throw new Error(String(x));
}
function sides(s: Shape): number {
  switch (s) {
    case Shape.Circle: return 0;
    case Shape.Square: return 4;
    default: return assertNever(s);
  }
}
Complexity: O(1)

