TypeScript · hashtable · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: Separate chaining keeps a list per bucket; storing one entry per bucket loses colliding keys
Misconception: Writing buckets[index] = [key, value] stores every key.
Explanation: Two keys can hash to the same index. A bucket that holds a single entry overwrites the first key when the second arrives. Chaining keeps a list per bucket and appends to it, so colliding keys coexist and lookups scan the short chain. The expected chain length is the load factor.
Incorrect:
type Table<K, V> = { buckets: [K, V][][]; count: number };
function putBad<V>(buckets: ([string, V] | undefined)[], k: string, v: V): void {
  buckets[hashString(k) % buckets.length] = [k, v];
}
Correct:
type Table<K, V> = { buckets: [K, V][][]; count: number };
function chainPut<V>(t: Table<string, V>, k: string, v: V): void {
  t.buckets[hashString(k) % t.buckets.length]!.push([k, v]);
  t.count++;
}
Complexity: O(1)

