TypeScript · heap · patterns · L3
Concept: The running median uses a max-heap for the lower half and a min-heap for the upper half, rebalanced after each insert
Misconception: Pushing every number into one heap and the next into the other keeps the median available.
Explanation: The two halves must stay balanced (sizes differ by at most one) and ordered (every lower value is at most every upper value). Insert into the lower half, move its maximum to the upper half, and move one back if the upper half becomes larger. Without the rebalancing the median is read from the wrong heap.
Incorrect:
class MedianBad {
  private lo: number[] = [];
  private hi: number[] = [];
  add(x: number): void {
    if (this.lo.length <= this.hi.length) this.lo.push(x);
    else this.hi.push(x);
  }
  median(): number {
    const lo = [...this.lo].sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const hi = [...this.hi].sort((a, b) => a - b);
    const l = lo[lo.length - 1] as number;
    return this.lo.length > this.hi.length ? l : (l + (hi[0] as number)) / 2;
  }
}
Correct:
class MedianFinder {
  private lo = new MinHeap();   // stores negated values: a max-heap
  private hi = new MinHeap();
  add(x: number): void {
    this.lo.push(-x);
    this.hi.push(-(this.lo.pop() as number));
    if (this.hi.size > this.lo.size) this.lo.push(-(this.hi.pop() as number));
  }
  median(): number {
    const l = -(this.lo.peek() as number);
    return this.lo.size > this.hi.size ? l : (l + (this.hi.peek() as number)) / 2;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n)

