Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · json · equality · L3
Concept: Equal objects can stringify differently; sort keys recursively for a stable string to compare or hash
Misconception: Two objects with the same fields always produce the same JSON string.
Explanation: stringify writes keys in insertion order, so {a:1,b:2} and {b:2,a:1} differ. For cache keys, hashes and equality checks, produce a canonical form: sort the keys at every level. Arrays keep their order.
Incorrect:
function keyBad(o: unknown): string {
  return JSON.stringify(o);
}
Correct:
function sortKeys(v: unknown): unknown {
  if (Array.isArray(v)) return v.map(sortKeys);
  if (typeof v === "object" && v !== null) {
    const o = v as Record<string, unknown>;
    return Object.fromEntries(Object.keys(o).sort().map((k) => [k, sortKeys(o[k])]));
  }
  return v;
}
function stableKey(o: unknown): string {
  return JSON.stringify(sortKeys(o));
}
Complexity: O(n log n)

