TypeScript · linkedlist · edge-cases · L3
Concept: Floyd cycle detection must move the pointers before comparing them
Misconception: Comparing slow and fast at the top of the loop detects a cycle.
Explanation: Both pointers start at the head, so comparing them before moving always succeeds for any list with at least two nodes. Move first (slow one step, fast two) and then compare. A list with no cycle ends when fast or fast.next becomes null, in O(n) time and O(1) space.
Incorrect:
type ListNode<T> = { value: T; next: ListNode<T> | null };
function hasCycleBad<T>(head: ListNode<T> | null): boolean {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;
  while (fast?.next) {
    if (slow === fast) return true;
    slow = slow!.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
  }
  return false;
}
Correct:
type ListNode<T> = { value: T; next: ListNode<T> | null };
function hasCycle<T>(head: ListNode<T> | null): boolean {
  let slow = head;
  let fast = head;
  while (fast?.next) {
    slow = slow!.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
Complexity: O(n)

