TypeScript · object · edge-cases · L1
Concept: A plain object inherits keys such as toString and constructor; use Object.hasOwn, a null-prototype object, or a Map
Misconception: An empty object literal has no keys, so counts[word] is undefined for a new word.
Explanation: Object literals inherit from Object.prototype, so counts["constructor"] and counts["toString"] are functions, not undefined. A word counter then computes "function Object() ..." plus 1. Check own properties with Object.hasOwn(obj, key), create the object with Object.create(null), or use a Map, which has no inherited keys.
