Concept: A comparator must return a number; returning a boolean is error TS2322 and breaks the order at run time
Misconception: (a, b) => a > b is a valid comparator.
Explanation: The comparator type is (a, b) => number. A boolean is not a number, so the compiler rejects it (TS2322). In plain JavaScript true and false convert to 1 and 0, so negative results never occur and the order is wrong. Use a - b for numbers, b - a for a max-first queue, and localeCompare for strings.
Incorrect (compile error TS2322):
const badPq = new PriorityQueue<number>((a, b) => a > b);
Correct:
const minPq = new PriorityQueue<number>((a, b) => a - b);
const maxPq = new PriorityQueue<number>((a, b) => b - a);
Complexity: O(1)

