Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · priorityqueue · creation · L0
Concept: A priority queue is a binary heap with a comparator; sorting with the default sort orders numbers as strings
Misconception: Keeping an array sorted with items.sort() always puts the smallest number first.
Explanation: Array.sort without a comparator converts values to strings, so 10 sorts before 9. Re-sorting on every push is also O(n log n) per insert. A binary heap keeps the smallest (by comparator) element at index 0, with O(log n) push and pop. The comparator returns negative, zero or positive, like Array.sort. Mnemonist (npm) has Heap, MinHeap and MaxHeap for the same job.
Incorrect:
class PqBad {
  private items: number[] = [];
  push(x: number): void {
    this.items.push(x);
    this.items.sort();
  }
  pop(): number | undefined {
    return this.items.shift();
  }
}
Correct:
class PriorityQueue<T> {
  private heap: T[] = [];
  constructor(private cmp: (a: T, b: T) => number) {}
  get size(): number { return this.heap.length; }
  peek(): T | undefined { return this.heap[0]; }
  push(x: T): void {
    const h = this.heap;
    h.push(x);
    let i = h.length - 1;
