TypeScript · queue · patterns · L3
Concept: A ring buffer needs a count (or a spare slot) to tell a full buffer from an empty one
Misconception: head === tail means the ring buffer is empty.
Explanation: In a circular array, head and tail are equal both when it is empty and when it is full after tail wraps. Without a separate count the two cases are indistinguishable. Keep an explicit count; enqueue fails (or overwrites) when count equals capacity.
Incorrect:
class RingBad {
  private data: number[];
  private head = 0;
  private tail = 0;
  constructor(private cap: number) { this.data = new Array<number>(cap); }
  push(x: number): void { this.data[this.tail] = x; this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.cap; }
  isEmpty(): boolean { return this.head === this.tail; }
}
Correct:
class Ring {
  private data: number[];
  private head = 0;
  private count = 0;
  constructor(private cap: number) { this.data = new Array<number>(cap); }
  push(x: number): boolean {
    if (this.count === this.cap) return false;
    this.data[(this.head + this.count++) % this.cap] = x;
    return true;
  }
  shift(): number | undefined {
    if (this.count === 0) return undefined;
    const x = this.data[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.cap;
    this.count--;
    return x;
  }
  isEmpty(): boolean { return this.count === 0; }
}
Complexity: O(1)

