Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · redblacktree · testing · L3
Concept: A validator must check equal black heights, not only the absence of red-red links
Misconception: No red node has a red child, so the tree is valid.
Explanation: Red-black trees need three things: a black root, no red node with a red child, and the same number of black nodes on every path from a node to a leaf. Checking only the second rule accepts a chain of black nodes. Return the black height from each subtree, or -1 when the subtree breaks a rule.
Incorrect:
function isValidBad<K>(n: RbNode<K> | null): boolean {
  if (n === null) return true;
  if (isRed(n) && (isRed(n.left) || isRed(n.right))) return false;
  return isValidBad(n.left) && isValidBad(n.right);
}
Correct:
function blackHeight<K>(n: RbNode<K> | null): number {
  if (n === null) return 1;
  if (isRed(n.right)) return -1;
  if (isRed(n) && isRed(n.left)) return -1;
  const l = blackHeight(n.left);
  const r = blackHeight(n.right);
  if (l < 0 || r < 0 || l !== r) return -1;
  return l + (isRed(n) ? 0 : 1);
}
function isValidRb<K>(root: RbNode<K> | null): boolean {
  return !isRed(root) && blackHeight(root) > 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

