Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · redblacktree · complexity · L3
Concept: Colour rules cap the height at 2 log2(n + 1); an unbalanced insert gives a chain
Misconception: Colouring nodes red and black is enough to keep the tree shallow.
Explanation: The colours only help if the fix-up runs. Because every path has the same number of black nodes and no path has two reds in a row, the longest path is at most twice the shortest, so the height is at most 2 log2(n + 1). Plain insertion of sorted keys with no fix-up builds a chain of height n.
Incorrect:
function insertPlain<K>(h: RbNode<K> | null, key: K, cmp: Cmp<K>): RbNode<K> {
  if (h === null) return new RbNode(key, BLACK);
  const c = cmp(key, h.key);
  if (c < 0) h.left = insertPlain(h.left, key, cmp);
  else if (c > 0) h.right = insertPlain(h.right, key, cmp);
  return h;
}
Correct:
function height<K>(n: RbNode<K> | null): number {
  return n === null ? 0 : 1 + Math.max(height(n.left), height(n.right));
}
function maxAllowed(n: number): number {
  return 2 * Math.log2(n + 1);
}
Complexity: O(log n)

