TypeScript · segmenttree · edge-cases · L3
Concept: The starting value of a min query is Infinity; 0 wins against every positive number
Misconception: A running minimum can start at 0.
Explanation: The identity of min is Infinity, because min(Infinity, x) is x. Starting from 0 returns 0 for any list of positive numbers. The same holds for padding unused slots of the tree. Use 0 for sum, 1 for product, Infinity for min and -Infinity for max.
Incorrect:
function minBad(t: number[], n: number, l: number, r: number): number {
  let res = 0;
  for (l += n, r += n; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
    if (l & 1) res = Math.min(res, t[l++] as number);
    if (r & 1) res = Math.min(res, t[--r] as number);
  }
  return res;
}
Correct:
class MinSeg {
  n: number;
  t: number[];
  constructor(a: number[]) {
    this.n = a.length;
    this.t = new Array<number>(2 * this.n).fill(Infinity);
    a.forEach((v, i) => { this.t[this.n + i] = v; });
    for (let i = this.n - 1; i >= 1; i--) {
      this.t[i] = Math.min(this.t[2 * i] as number, this.t[2 * i + 1] as number);
    }
  }
  query(l: number, r: number): number {
    let res = Infinity;
    for (l += this.n, r += this.n; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
      if (l & 1) res = Math.min(res, this.t[l++] as number);
      if (r & 1) res = Math.min(res, this.t[--r] as number);
    }
    return res;
  }
}
Complexity: O(log n)

