TypeScript · set · patterns · L2
Concept: To keep the first item for each key, track seen keys in a Set; a Map built from pairs keeps the last
Misconception: new Map(items.map(i => [key(i), i])).values() keeps the first item per key.
Explanation: When a Map is built from pairs, later pairs overwrite earlier ones, so it keeps the last item for each key. A filter with a Set of seen keys keeps the first and preserves the original order.
Incorrect:
function uniqueByBad<T>(xs: readonly T[], key: (x: T) => string): T[] {
  return [...new Map(xs.map((x) => [key(x), x] as const)).values()];
}
Correct:
function uniqueBy<T>(xs: readonly T[], key: (x: T) => string): T[] {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  return xs.filter((x) => {
    const k = key(x);
    if (seen.has(k)) return false;
    seen.add(k);
    return true;
  });
}
Complexity: O(n)

