Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · stack · patterns · L2
Concept: Balanced brackets need a stack of expected closers; a counter cannot detect mismatched kinds
Misconception: Counting opening and closing brackets is enough to validate nesting.
Explanation: A single counter accepts "([)]" because the counts match, but the nesting is wrong. A stack remembers the order of open brackets: on a closer, the top of the stack must be the matching opener. The string is balanced only if the stack is empty at the end.
Incorrect:
function balancedBad(s: string): boolean {
  let n = 0;
  for (const c of s) {
    if ("([{".includes(c)) n++;
    else if (")]}".includes(c)) n--;
    if (n < 0) return false;
  }
  return n === 0;
}
Correct:
function balanced(s: string): boolean {
  const pairs: Record<string, string> = { ")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{" };
  const stack: string[] = [];
  for (const c of s) {
    if ("([{".includes(c)) stack.push(c);
    else if (c in pairs && stack.pop() !== pairs[c]) return false;
  }
  return stack.length === 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

