Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · trie · insertion-and-deletion · L1
Concept: Insert reuses an existing child node; creating a new node at every character erases earlier words
Misconception: insert can create a fresh node for each character of the word.
Explanation: Words share their prefixes, which is the point of a trie. Insert must look for an existing child first and create one only when it is missing. Replacing the child at every character overwrites the subtree that earlier words built, so inserting "app" after "apple" removes "apple".
Incorrect:
function insertBad(t: Trie, word: string): void {
  let node = t.root;
  for (const ch of word) {
    const next = new TrieNode();
    node.children.set(ch, next);
    node = next;
  }
  node.end = true;
}
Correct:
class TrieNode {
  children = new Map<string, TrieNode>();
  end = false;
}
class Trie {
  root = new TrieNode();
  insert(word: string): void {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      let next = node.children.get(ch);
      if (!next) {
        next = new TrieNode();
        node.children.set(ch, next);
      }
      node = next;
    }
    node.end = true;
  }
  find(prefix: string): TrieNode | undefined {
    let node: TrieNode | undefined = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      if (!node) return undefined;
    }
    return node;
  }
  has(word: string): boolean { return this.find(word)?.end === true; }
  startsWith(prefix: string): boolean { return this.find(prefix) !== undefined; }
}
Complexity: O(L)

