Imported reference; not locally executed. Subject: typescript
TypeScript · tuple · access · L1
Concept: Destructuring an array gives string, not string | undefined; check the length or use a tuple
Misconception: const [a, b] = xs always gives two strings when xs is a string[].
Explanation: For an array type the compiler assumes every element exists, so b has type string even when the array has one element, and the result is "undefined" at run time. A tuple type or a length check proves the shape. The compiler option noUncheckedIndexedAccess adds undefined to array reads.
Incorrect:
function joinBad(xs: string[]): string {
  const [a, b] = xs;
  return a + b;
}
Correct:
function join(xs: string[]): string | null {
  if (xs.length < 2) return null;
  const [a, b] = xs as [string, string];
  return a + b;
}
Complexity: O(1)

