TypeScript · tuple · access · L0
Concept: A tuple has a fixed length: reading an index past the end is error TS2493
Misconception: A tuple behaves like an array, so t[2] on a two-element tuple is undefined and allowed.
Explanation: The tuple type [number, string] knows it has exactly two elements. Reading t[2] is error TS2493 ("Tuple type of length 2 has no element at index 2"), unlike a number[] where any index is accepted. That is the main benefit of tuples: wrong positions are caught at compile time. At run time a tuple is an ordinary array.
Incorrect (compile error TS2493):
function thirdBad(t: [number, string]): unknown {
  return t[2];
}
Correct:
function second(t: [number, string]): string {
  return t[1];
}
Complexity: O(1)

