Concept: Tuples are arrays, so a Set or Map keyed by a tuple compares references; key by a string
Misconception: new Set([[1, 2]]).has([1, 2]) is true.
Explanation: Two tuple literals are different objects, so Set.has and Map.get never find an equal-looking key. For grids and coordinate pairs, build a string key such as `${x},${y}` or a numeric key, and convert back when needed.
Incorrect:
function visitedBad(seen: Set<[number, number]>, x: number, y: number): boolean {
  return seen.has([x, y]);
}
Correct:
function visited(seen: Set<string>, x: number, y: number): boolean {
  return seen.has(`${x},${y}`);
}
Complexity: O(1)

