TypeScript · tuple · mutation · L1
Concept: TypeScript allows push on a tuple, so the runtime length can differ from the type; return a new tuple instead
Misconception: A [number, number] tuple cannot grow, because its length is part of the type.
Explanation: Methods such as push, pop and splice are still available on a mutable tuple and the compiler does not track them. After t.push(3) the value has three elements while its type says two. Treat tuples as immutable: declare them readonly, or build a new tuple with spread and an explicit return type.
Incorrect:
function addBad(t: [number, number]): [number, number] {
  t.push(9);
  return t;
}
Correct:
function add(t: readonly [number, number]): [number, number, number] {
  return [...t, 9];
}
Complexity: O(1)

