TypeScript · typealias · creation · L3
Concept: A recursive alias must recurse through an object or array; a direct self-reference is error TS2456
Misconception: type Json = Json | string is a valid recursive type.
Explanation: An alias that refers to itself directly in a union has no structure and is error TS2456. Recursion is allowed inside object properties, array elements and tuples, which is how a JSON value type is written.
Incorrect (compile error TS2456):
type LoopB = LoopB | string;
Correct:
type Json = string | number | boolean | null | Json[] | { [k: string]: Json };
function depth(j: Json): number {
  if (Array.isArray(j)) return 1 + Math.max(0, ...j.map(depth));
  if (typeof j === "object" && j !== null) {
    return 1 + Math.max(0, ...Object.values(j).map(depth));
  }
  return 0;
}
Complexity: O(n)

