TypeScript · weakset · patterns · L2
Concept: Use a fresh WeakSet per traversal to mark visited nodes and survive cycles
Misconception: Marking nodes with a visited property is the same as using a visited set.
Explanation: A property on the node persists after the traversal, so the next traversal thinks every node is already visited. A per-call WeakSet starts empty each time, handles cycles, and leaves the nodes unchanged. Use a Set when you also need the visit order or a count.
Incorrect:
type BNode = { id: number; next: BNode[]; seen?: boolean };
function walkBad(n: BNode, out: number[] = []): number[] {
  if (n.seen) return out;
  n.seen = true;
  out.push(n.id);
  for (const m of n.next) walkBad(m, out);
  return out;
}
Correct:
type GNode = { id: number; next: GNode[]; seen?: boolean };
function walk(n: GNode, seen: WeakSet<GNode> = new WeakSet(), out: number[] = []): number[] {
  if (seen.has(n)) return out;
  seen.add(n);
  out.push(n.id);
  for (const m of n.next) walk(m, seen, out);
  return out;
}
Complexity: O(V+E)

